results.statement:
\label{defn:disjoint-union}\index{disjoint union}
    Let $(X_\alpha)_{\alpha \in A}$ be a family of sets over a non-empty set $A$. The set
    \begin{align*}
        \bigsqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha = \bigcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\} = \bigcup_{\alpha \in A} \{(x,\alpha) \mid x \in X_\alpha \}
    \end{align*}
    is called the disjoint union of $(X_\alpha)_{\alpha \in A}$.

\label{exmp:adm-static}\index{admissible orders}\index{static market}
    Consider a simple static market, in which each order can be represented by $(p, q)$ where $p \in \mathbb{R}$ is the price\footnote{Classically, one often assumes prices to be positive, but in power markets prices may be negative. Real venues also impose product-specific price ticks and admissible price ranges; for European spot power examples, see \citet{epex2026tradingproducts,nemocommitteeSIDC}.} and $q \in [0,\infty)$ is the quantity\footnote{Like prices, actual order quantities are venue- and product-specific and may be restricted by minimum volume increments or lot sizes \citep{epex2026tradingproducts,nemocommitteeSIDC}.}. Each order can be classified as a buy or sell order, such that $((p,q), \kappa)$ is an order with direction $\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}$. As such, the space of admissible orders may be given by
    \begin{align*}
        \bigsqcup_{\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}} \mathbb{R}\times [0,\infty).
    \end{align*}
    However, in this example, there is no difference between using disjoint unions or the product space
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{R}\times [0,\infty) \times \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}.
    \end{align*}
    Indeed, one defines the map 
    \begin{align*}
        ((p,q), \kappa) \mapsto (p,q,\kappa),
    \end{align*}
    which is easily shown to be an isomorphism. In this example, one may view the disjoint union as a nuisance, but, as we shall later see, it has advantages.

\label{exmp:agent-adm-static}
We now consider a slight modification of Example~\ref{exmp:adm-static}, where one considers a set of agents, $A$, who can engage with the market. Suppose that the admissible orders of each agent are limited in price and quantity based on direction. The corresponding bounds may vary across agents.

    Such a scenario may be covered by letting $P_{\kappa, a}\subseteq \mathbb{R}$ and $Q_{\kappa, a}\subseteq [0,\infty)$ be the sets of potential admissible prices and quantities for agent $a\in A$ when taking positions in direction $\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}$. Therefore, each order can be seen as an element of the space 
    \begin{align*}
        \bigsqcup_{(\kappa, a) \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}\times A} P_{\kappa, a} \times Q_{\kappa, a}. 
    \end{align*}
    The mapping
    \begin{align}\label{eq:agent-adm-static-inj}
        ((p,q),(\kappa, a)) \mapsto (p,q,\kappa, a)
    \end{align}
    is generally no longer a bijection, although it is still injective. If it is not a bijection, then
    \begin{align*}
        \bigsqcup_{(\kappa, a) \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}\times A} P_{\kappa, a} \times Q_{\kappa, a} \subsetneq \mathbb{R}\times [0,\infty) \times \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}\times A,
    \end{align*}
    where the strict inclusion is meant under the map \eqref{eq:agent-adm-static-inj}.

\label{exmp:agent-adm-dyn}
    We now extend Example~\ref{exmp:agent-adm-static} to the dynamic case. Once again, let $A$ be the set of agents within the market, and let $P_{\kappa, a, t}$ and $Q_{\kappa, a, t}$ be the sets of prices and quantities that are admissible for agent $a \in A$ at time $t \in [0,\infty)$ in direction $\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}$. Each order can be seen as an element of
    \begin{align*}
        \bigsqcup_{(\kappa, a, t) \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}\times A\times [0,\infty) } P_{\kappa, a, t}\times Q_{\kappa, a, t}.
    \end{align*}
    Again, the map
    \begin{align*}
        ((p,q),(\kappa, a, t)) \mapsto (p,q,\kappa,a,t),
    \end{align*}
    is injective. It gives the canonical injections
    \begin{align}
        &\bigsqcup_{(\kappa, a, t) \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}\times A\times [0,\infty) } P_{\kappa, a, t}\times Q_{\kappa, a, t}\label{eq:agent-adm-dyn-1} \\
        &\hookrightarrow  \bigsqcup_{(\kappa, a) \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}\times A} P_{\kappa, a}\times Q_{\kappa, a} \times [0,\infty)\label{eq:agent-adm-dyn-2}\\
        &\hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty) \times \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\} \times A \times [0,\infty),\label{eq:agent-adm-dyn-3} 
    \end{align}
    where
    \begin{align*}
        P_{\kappa, a} \equiv \bigcup_{t\in [0,\infty)}P_{\kappa, a, t} \quad \quad Q_{\kappa, a}\equiv \bigcup_{t\in [0,\infty)}Q_{\kappa, a, t}.
    \end{align*}
    In general, the set in \eqref{eq:agent-adm-dyn-1} restricts the trading possibilities of each agent in each direction at each time, whereas \eqref{eq:agent-adm-dyn-2} does not have time-varying bounds for each agent and direction. These examples are relevant for deterministic time-varying restrictions, such as gate closure or time-specific price limits; state-dependent admissibility, for instance when an agent has already taken a short position and cannot sell short further because of margin constraints, is modelled as a constraint on actions given the current state. Finally, \eqref{eq:agent-adm-dyn-3} is the agent-labelled version of the unrestricted order space of Example~\ref{exmp:adm-dyn}.

steps.text:
We must show that there exists an isomorphism between the sets $\bigcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$ and $\bigsqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$. Let $\iota : \bigsqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha \to \bigcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$ be the mapping defined by 
    \begin{align*}
        \iota(x,\alpha) = x.
    \end{align*}
    We show that $\iota$ is bijective. For injectivity, we require
    \begin{align*}
        \iota(x,\alpha) = \iota(x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) \implies (x, \alpha) = (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime),
    \end{align*}
    and indeed, if $\iota(x,\alpha) = \iota(x^\prime,\alpha^\prime)$, then $x = x^\prime$; since the sets are pairwise disjoint, $x \in X_\alpha \cap X_{\alpha^\prime}$ forces $\alpha = \alpha^\prime$. Hence $\iota$ is injective, and it is clearly surjective. Therefore, it is bijective and hence an isomorphism between the disjoint union and ordinary union, when the components are disjoint.

