results.statement:
\label{prop:disjoint-partial-order}\index{partial order}
    Let $(X_\alpha, \preceq_\alpha)_{\alpha \in A}$ be a family of partially ordered sets. Let $X = \sqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$ and define the relation $\preceq$ on $X$ by
    \begin{align*}
        (x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) \iff (\alpha = \alpha^\prime) \land x \preceq_\alpha x^\prime.
    \end{align*}
    Then $(X,\preceq)$ is a partially ordered set.

\label{exmp:partial-order-static-market}
    Let us consider the setting of Example~\ref{exmp:adm-static}. Now, define 
    \begin{align*}
        (p,q) \preceq_{\mathrm{buy}} (p^\prime, q^\prime) \iff (p > p^\prime) \lor ((p = p^\prime) \land (q \geq q^\prime)), 
    \end{align*}
    which states that a buy order is ``better'' if the price per unit is greater, or if the prices are equal and more volume is provided. Similarly, 
    \begin{align*}
        (p,q) \preceq_{\mathrm{sell}} (p^\prime, q^\prime) \iff (p < p^\prime) \lor ((p = p^\prime) \land (q \geq q^\prime)), 
    \end{align*}
    that is, a sell order is ``better'' if the price per unit is smaller or the price per unit is equal but more volume is provided. The orderings rank orders by priority, and in both cases the order on the left-hand side has priority over the one on the right-hand side. The resulting ordering on the disjoint union allows us to compare orders of the same type, but buy and sell orders are, once again, incomparable.

steps.text:
We must show for all $(x,\alpha),(x^\prime,\alpha^\prime), (x^\ast, \alpha^\ast)$ that 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x,\alpha)$. \label{property:disjoint-total-order-1}
        \item $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\prime,\alpha^\prime)$ and $(x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) \preceq (x^\ast, \alpha^\ast)$ implies $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\ast, \alpha^\ast)$. \label{property:disjoint-total-order-2}
        \item $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\prime ,\alpha^\prime )$ and $(x^\prime ,\alpha^\prime ) \preceq (x,\alpha)$ implies $(x,\alpha) =(x^\prime,\alpha^\prime)$. \label{property:disjoint-total-order-3}
        \item $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)$ or $(x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) \preceq (x,\alpha)$. \label{property:disjoint-total-order-4}
    \end{enumerate}
    Note that \eqref{property:disjoint-total-order-1} is $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x,\alpha)$ if and only if $(\alpha < \alpha) \lor ((\alpha =\alpha) \land (x \preceq_\alpha x))$ which holds if and only if $x \preceq_\alpha x$. However, since each $\preceq_\alpha$ is a total order the property follows.

Finally, we must show that $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)$ or $(x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) \preceq (x,\alpha)$. To do so, notice that 
    \begin{align*}
        &((x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) )\lor ((x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) \preceq (x,\alpha))\iff\\ &(\alpha < \alpha^\prime) \lor (\alpha^\prime < \alpha) \lor ((\alpha = \alpha^\prime) \land ((x \preceq_\alpha x^\prime) \lor (x^\prime \preceq_\alpha x))),
    \end{align*}
    and the three mutually exclusive cases are $\alpha < \alpha^\prime$, $\alpha^\prime < \alpha$, and $\alpha = \alpha^\prime$. Since $\leq$ is total on $A$, exactly one of them holds, and in the third case the disjunction holds because $\preceq_\alpha$ is total. For the antisymmetric relation $\preceq$, this connexity property is equivalent to the totality condition~(iv) of Definition~\ref{defn:ordered-sets}.

proof.text:
We must show for all $(x,\alpha),(x^\prime,\alpha^\prime), (x^\ast, \alpha^\ast)$ that 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x,\alpha)$. \label{property:disjoint-total-order-1}
        \item $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\prime,\alpha^\prime)$ and $(x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) \preceq (x^\ast, \alpha^\ast)$ implies $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\ast, \alpha^\ast)$. \label{property:disjoint-total-order-2}
        \item $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\prime ,\alpha^\prime )$ and $(x^\prime ,\alpha^\prime ) \preceq (x,\alpha)$ implies $(x,\alpha) =(x^\prime,\alpha^\prime)$. \label{property:disjoint-total-order-3}
        \item $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)$ or $(x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) \preceq (x,\alpha)$. \label{property:disjoint-total-order-4}
    \end{enumerate}
    Note that \eqref{property:disjoint-total-order-1} is $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x,\alpha)$ if and only if $(\alpha < \alpha) \lor ((\alpha =\alpha) \land (x \preceq_\alpha x))$ which holds if and only if $x \preceq_\alpha x$. However, since each $\preceq_\alpha$ is a total order the property follows. 

    For transitivity, suppose $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\prime,\alpha^\prime)$ and $(x^\prime,\alpha^\prime) \preceq (x^\ast,\alpha^\ast)$. If either $\alpha<\alpha^\prime$ or $\alpha^\prime<\alpha^\ast$, then transitivity of the total order on $A$ gives $\alpha\leq\alpha^\ast$; moreover, $\alpha \neq \alpha^\ast$, since $\alpha = \alpha^\ast$ would give $\alpha \leq \alpha^\prime \leq \alpha$ with one inequality strict, contradicting antisymmetry. Hence $\alpha<\alpha^\ast$. In the remaining case $\alpha=\alpha^\prime=\alpha^\ast$, transitivity of $\preceq_\alpha$ gives $x\preceq_\alpha x^\ast$. Thus $(x,\alpha)\preceq (x^\ast,\alpha^\ast)$. For antisymmetry, if both $(x,\alpha)\preceq (x^\prime,\alpha^\prime)$ and $(x^\prime,\alpha^\prime)\preceq (x,\alpha)$ hold, then total-order antisymmetry on $A$ forces $\alpha=\alpha^\prime$, and antisymmetry of $\preceq_\alpha$ then forces $x=x^\prime$. Hence $(x,\alpha)=(x^\prime,\alpha^\prime)$. 

    Finally, we must show that $(x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)$ or $(x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) \preceq (x,\alpha)$. To do so, notice that 
    \begin{align*}
        &((x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) )\lor ((x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) \preceq (x,\alpha))\iff\\ &(\alpha < \alpha^\prime) \lor (\alpha^\prime < \alpha) \lor ((\alpha = \alpha^\prime) \land ((x \preceq_\alpha x^\prime) \lor (x^\prime \preceq_\alpha x))),
    \end{align*}
    and the three mutually exclusive cases are $\alpha < \alpha^\prime$, $\alpha^\prime < \alpha$, and $\alpha = \alpha^\prime$. Since $\leq$ is total on $A$, exactly one of them holds, and in the third case the disjunction holds because $\preceq_\alpha$ is total. For the antisymmetric relation $\preceq$, this connexity property is equivalent to the totality condition~(iv) of Definition~\ref{defn:ordered-sets}.

results.statement:
\label{prop:disjoint-total-order}\index{lexicographic order}
    Let $(A, \leq)$ be a totally ordered set and let $(X_\alpha, \preceq_\alpha)_{\alpha \in A}$ be a family of totally ordered sets. Let $X = \sqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$ and define the relation $\preceq$ on $X$ by
    \begin{align*}
        (x,\alpha) \preceq (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime) \iff (\alpha < \alpha^\prime) \lor (\alpha = \alpha^\prime \land x \preceq_\alpha x^\prime).
    \end{align*}
    Then $(X,\preceq)$ is a totally ordered set.

We consider the non-strict case in Proposition~\ref{prop:disjoint-total-order}. However, one can proceed analogously with the strict case $\prec$; the two differ only by the diagonal $\Delta_X = \{(x,x) \mid x \in X\}$. That is,
    \begin{align*}
        \prec ~=~ \preceq\setminus\Delta_X,
    \end{align*}
    and 
    \begin{align*}
        \preceq ~= ~ \prec\cup ~\Delta_X,
    \end{align*}
    where one has to recall that a relation is, strictly speaking, a subset of $X \times X$.

\label{cor:strict-disjoint-total-order}
    Let $(A, <)$ be a strictly totally ordered set (that is, $<$ is irreflexive and transitive, and $a \neq a^\prime$ implies $a < a^\prime$ or $a^\prime < a$) and let $(X_\alpha, \prec_\alpha)_{\alpha \in A}$ be a family of strictly totally ordered sets. Let $X = \sqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$ and define the order $\prec$ on $X$ by
    \begin{align*}
        (x_\alpha,\alpha) \prec (x_{\alpha^\prime}^\prime, \alpha^\prime) \iff (\alpha < \alpha^\prime) \lor (\alpha = \alpha^\prime \land x_\alpha \prec_\alpha x_{\alpha^\prime}^\prime).
    \end{align*}
    Then $(X,\prec)$ is a strictly totally ordered set.

\label{thm:szpilrajn}\index{Szpilrajn extension theorem}
    Let $(X, \preceq)$ be a partially ordered set. Then, there exists a total order, $\leq$, on $X$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \forall x,x^\prime\in X : x \preceq x^\prime \implies x \leq x^\prime.
    \end{align*}

\label{exmp:total-order-static-market}
    We continue from Examples~\ref{exmp:adm-static} and \ref{exmp:partial-order-static-market} in which we considered the orders as elements of the set 
    \begin{align*}
        O = \bigsqcup_{\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}} \mathbb{R}\times [0,\infty),  
    \end{align*}
    where we endowed the buy and sell orders with the two orders $\preceq_{\mathrm{buy}}$ and $\preceq_{\mathrm{sell}}$ of Example~\ref{exmp:partial-order-static-market}.
    Endow $\{\mathrm{buy}, ~\mathrm{sell}\}$ with the (arbitrarily chosen) total order $\leq$ in which buy precedes sell, given by
    \begin{align*}
        \kappa \leq \kappa^\prime \iff \kappa = \kappa^\prime \lor (\kappa = \mathrm{buy} \land \kappa^\prime = \mathrm{sell}).
    \end{align*}
    Since $\leq, \preceq_{\mathrm{buy}}$, and  $\preceq_{\mathrm{sell}}$ are all total orders, we have that the lexicographic order defined by
    \begin{align*}
        (o, \kappa) \preceq (o^\prime, \kappa^\prime) \iff \kappa < \kappa^\prime \lor (\kappa = \kappa^\prime \land o \preceq_\kappa o^\prime), 
    \end{align*}
    is total, where $<$ is the strict part of $\leq$, $(o,\kappa) =((p,q),\kappa)\in O$ and $(o^\prime, \kappa^\prime)=((p^\prime,q^\prime),\kappa^\prime) \in O$.

\label{exmp:total-order-dynamic-market}
    We continue from Example~\ref{exmp:adm-dyn}, in which we considered the orders as elements of the set 
    \begin{align*}
        O = \bigsqcup_{\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}} \mathbb{R}\times [0,\infty) \times [0,\infty),  
    \end{align*}
    where the components of each $((p,q,t), \kappa) \in O$ represent the price, $p \in \mathbb{R}$, quantity, $q \in [0,\infty)$, time of submission, $t \in [0,\infty)$, and direction $\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},\mathrm{sell}\}$. Now, define the orders 
    \begin{align*}
        &(p,q,t) \preceq_{\mathrm{buy}} (p^\prime, q^\prime, t^\prime) \iff\\ &(p > p^\prime) \lor ((p = p^\prime) \land (t < t^\prime)) \lor ((p = p^\prime) \land (t = t^\prime) \land (q \geq q^\prime)),
    \end{align*}
    which ranks a buy order $(p,q,t)$ ahead of $(p^\prime, q^\prime, t^\prime)$ if it has a higher price; at equal prices, if it was submitted earlier; and at equal prices and times, if it is for a larger quantity. Similarly, we define 
    \begin{align*}
        &(p,q, t) \preceq_{\mathrm{sell}} (p^\prime, q^\prime,t^\prime) \iff \\
        &(p < p^\prime) \lor ((p = p^\prime) \land (t < t^\prime)) \lor ((p = p^\prime) \land (t = t^\prime) \land (q \geq q^\prime)),
    \end{align*}
    where sell orders are ranked increasingly according to price. That is, the ``best'' sell order is the one with the smallest price, but otherwise it is constructed analogously to the buy order. Endow $\{\mathrm{buy}, ~\mathrm{sell}\}$ with the order $\leq$ of Example~\ref{exmp:total-order-static-market}, for which
    \begin{align*}
        \kappa \leq \kappa^\prime \iff \kappa = \kappa^\prime \lor (\kappa = \mathrm{buy} \land \kappa^\prime = \mathrm{sell}).
    \end{align*}
    Since $\leq, \preceq_{\mathrm{buy}}$, and  $\preceq_{\mathrm{sell}}$ are all total orders, we have that the lexicographic order defined by
    \begin{align*}
        (o, \kappa) \preceq (o^\prime, \kappa^\prime) \iff \kappa < \kappa^\prime \lor (\kappa = \kappa^\prime \land o \preceq_\kappa o^\prime), 
    \end{align*}
    is total, where $<$ is the strict part of $\leq$ and $(o,\kappa),(o^\prime, \kappa^\prime) \in O$.

\label{exmp:completing-order-agent-based-static-market}\index{order completion}
    We continue from Example~\ref{exmp:agent-adm-static}, in which we considered the orders as elements of the set 
    \begin{align*}
        O = \bigsqcup_{(\kappa, a) \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}\times A} P_{\kappa, a} \times Q_{\kappa, a},  
    \end{align*}
    where $A$ is the set of agents in the market, and $P_{\kappa,a}\subseteq\mathbb{R}$ is the set of permissible prices in direction $\kappa\in\{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}$ for agent $a\in A$, and similarly $Q_{\kappa,a}\subseteq [0,\infty)$ is the set of permissible quantities in direction $\kappa\in\{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}$ for agent $a\in A$. Notice that attempting to follow a construction analogous to that in Example~\ref{exmp:total-order-static-market} is not satisfactory; endowing $\{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}\times A$ with a total order has the adverse consequence of favouring certain agents in the market. To rectify this, one may consider the equivalence relation $\sim$ on $O$ defined by 
    \begin{align*}
        ((p,q),(\kappa, a)) \sim ((p^\prime,q^\prime), (\kappa^\prime, a^\prime)) \iff ((p,q),\kappa) = ((p^\prime,q^\prime), \kappa^\prime),
    \end{align*}
    which states that two orders are equivalent if their price, quantity, and direction coincide, regardless of the agent that submitted the order. It is therefore natural for the market to consider the quotient space
    \begin{align*}
        O_{\sim} = \{[((p,q),\kappa,a)]_{\sim} \mid ((p,q), \kappa, a) \in O\}, 
    \end{align*}
    where 
    \begin{align*}
        [((p,q),\kappa,a)]_{\sim} = \{((p^\ast, q^\ast), (\kappa^\ast, a^\ast)) \in O  \mid ((p^\ast, q^\ast), (\kappa^\ast, a^\ast))\sim ((p,q),(\kappa,a))\}.
    \end{align*}
    The map
    \begin{align*}
        \pi&:O_\sim \to \bigsqcup_{\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}} \left(\bigcup_{a \in A} P_{\kappa, a} \times Q_{\kappa, a}\right)\\
        [((p,q),(\kappa,a))]_{\sim} &\mapsto ((p,q),\kappa)
    \end{align*}
    is well-defined and bijective. Therefore, one can conclude that 
    \begin{align*}
        O_{\sim} \cong \bigsqcup_{\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}} \left(\bigcup_{a \in A} P_{\kappa, a} \times Q_{\kappa, a}\right) \subseteq \bigsqcup_{\kappa \in \{\mathrm{buy},~\mathrm{sell}\}} \mathbb{R}\times [0,\infty).
    \end{align*}
Pulling back the lexicographic order of Example~\ref{exmp:total-order-static-market} through $\pi$ yields a total order on $O_{\sim}$.

\label{defn:disjoint-union-metrisation}\index{disjoint union!metric}
    Let $(X_\alpha, d_\alpha)_{\alpha \in A}$ be a family of metric spaces and $X = \sqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$. Define the mapping $d:X\times X\to [0,\infty]$ by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:disjoint-metric}
        d((x,\alpha), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)) = \begin{cases}
            d_\alpha(x,x^\prime) & \alpha = \alpha^\prime\\
            \infty & \alpha \neq \alpha^\prime.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align}
    We call $d$ the disjoint-union metric induced by $(d_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$, or simply the disjoint-union metric if the metrics $(d_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ are clear from context. Moreover, we refer to $(X,d)$ as the disjoint union metric space.

Note that we allow the metric to have range $[0,\infty]$, as opposed to $[0,\infty)$, which is utilised in classical analysis. In some texts, our definition is that of an \emph{extended} metric, whereas a metric requires the range to be $[0,\infty)$. For our purposes, this has no topological implication, because one can define the finite-valued metric
    \begin{align*}
        \bar{d}(x,x^\prime) =\min(d(x,x^\prime),1)
    \end{align*}
    which induces the same topology as its extended-valued counterpart (see \citet{munkres2000topology}).

steps.text:
We must show that the mapping $d:X\times X \to [0,\infty]$ satisfies the three criteria for all $(x,\alpha),(x^\prime, \alpha^\prime),(x^\ast, \alpha^\ast) \in X = \bigsqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$:
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item $d((x,\alpha), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)) = 0$ if and only if $(x,\alpha) = (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)$.\label{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-1}
        \item $d((x,\alpha), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)) = d((x^\prime, \alpha^\prime), (x,\alpha))$. \label{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-2}
        \item $d((x,\alpha), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)) \leq d((x,\alpha), (x^\ast, \alpha^\ast)) + d((x^\ast, \alpha^\ast), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime))$. \label{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-3}
    \end{enumerate}
    The criteria \eqref{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-1} and \eqref{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-2} are trivial. For \eqref{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-3}, we use the usual extended-real convention that $r\leq\infty$ for every $r\in[0,\infty]$. If $\alpha \neq \alpha^\prime$, the right-hand side of the inequality is infinite, since $\alpha^\ast$ cannot be equal to both $\alpha$ and $\alpha^\prime$. Hence, it remains to consider the case $\alpha = \alpha^\prime$. Again, if $\alpha^\ast \neq \alpha = \alpha^\prime$ the claim is trivial, so we only need to consider $\alpha^\ast = \alpha = \alpha^\prime$, but then it reduces to the standard triangle inequality for $d_\alpha$, which, by assumption, is a metric.

proof.text:
We must show that the mapping $d:X\times X \to [0,\infty]$ satisfies the three criteria for all $(x,\alpha),(x^\prime, \alpha^\prime),(x^\ast, \alpha^\ast) \in X = \bigsqcup_{\alpha \in A} X_\alpha$:
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item $d((x,\alpha), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)) = 0$ if and only if $(x,\alpha) = (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)$.\label{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-1}
        \item $d((x,\alpha), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)) = d((x^\prime, \alpha^\prime), (x,\alpha))$. \label{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-2}
        \item $d((x,\alpha), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime)) \leq d((x,\alpha), (x^\ast, \alpha^\ast)) + d((x^\ast, \alpha^\ast), (x^\prime, \alpha^\prime))$. \label{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-3}
    \end{enumerate}
    The criteria \eqref{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-1} and \eqref{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-2} are trivial. For \eqref{eq:disjoint-union-metrisation-3}, we use the usual extended-real convention that $r\leq\infty$ for every $r\in[0,\infty]$. If $\alpha \neq \alpha^\prime$, the right-hand side of the inequality is infinite, since $\alpha^\ast$ cannot be equal to both $\alpha$ and $\alpha^\prime$. Hence, it remains to consider the case $\alpha = \alpha^\prime$. Again, if $\alpha^\ast \neq \alpha = \alpha^\prime$ the claim is trivial, so we only need to consider $\alpha^\ast = \alpha = \alpha^\prime$, but then it reduces to the standard triangle inequality for $d_\alpha$, which, by assumption, is a metric.

steps.text:
First, we show \eqref{property:disjoint-metric-1}. We must show that there is a countable and dense subset $U \subseteq X$. Now, by assumption each $(X_\alpha, d_\alpha)$ is separable. Then, by definition, there exists a countable and dense subset, say $U_\alpha$, for which 
    \begin{align*}
        \forall x_\alpha \in X_\alpha\ \forall \varepsilon> 0\ \exists u_\alpha \in U_\alpha : d_\alpha(x_\alpha, u_\alpha) < \varepsilon.
    \end{align*}
    We claim that the set
    \begin{align*}
        U = \bigsqcup_{\alpha \in A} U_\alpha
    \end{align*}
    is both countable and dense in $(X,d)$. First, note that 
    \begin{align*}
        U = \bigcup_{\alpha \in A} (U_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}).
    \end{align*}
    For each $\alpha \in A$, the set $U_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}$ is countable, as it naturally bijects with the countable set $U_\alpha$. Moreover, $\bigcup_{\alpha \in A} U_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}$ is a countable union of countable sets and therefore countable. Hence, $U$ is indeed countable.

Next, we must show that any Cauchy sequence in $(X,d)$ converges in $X$. To this end, recall that a sequence $(x_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}} \subseteq X$ is said to be Cauchy if 
    \begin{align*}
        \forall \varepsilon > 0\ \exists N \in \mathbb{N}: m,n\geq N \implies d(x_n, x_m) < \varepsilon.
    \end{align*}
    Each $x \in X$ has the form $x = (x_\alpha, \alpha)$, and so the Cauchy sequence $(x_n)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ admits the representation 
    \begin{align*}
        x_n = (x_{\alpha_n, n}, \alpha_n).
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, since the sequence is Cauchy, choose $N \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $d(x_n,x_m)<1$ for all $n,m\geq N$. This finite bound implies $\alpha_n=\alpha_m$ for all $n,m\geq N$; set $\alpha=\alpha_N$. Now, for all tail indices, $d(x_n, x_m) = d_\alpha (x_{\alpha, n}, x_{\alpha,m})$, implying that the sequence $(x_{\alpha, n})_{n = N}^\infty$ is Cauchy in $X_\alpha$. By assumption, $(X_\alpha, d_\alpha)$ is complete, and so the sequence $(x_{\alpha, n})_{n = N}^\infty$ has a limit, say, $x_\alpha \in X_\alpha$. Then $x = (x_\alpha, \alpha) \in X$ and clearly $(x_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ converges to $x$.

