\label{prop:finite-set-cauchy}
    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space. Then, if $(x_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}} \subseteq X$ is a Cauchy sequence, the sequence $(\xi_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}} \subseteq \mathscr{F}(X)$ defined by
    \begin{align*}
        \xi_{n} = \bigcup_{i = 1}^n \{x_i\} 
    \end{align*}
    is a Cauchy sequence in $(\mathscr{F}(X), d_H)$.

[Definition~1.1]{lagarias1996meyer}}]\label{defn:uniformly-discrete}\index{uniformly discrete spaces}
    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space. We call $(X,d)$ \emph{uniformly discrete} if there exists a $\beta > 0$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \forall x, x^\prime \in X: x \neq x^\prime \implies d(x,x^\prime) \geq \beta.
    \end{align*}

steps.text:
First, let $(x_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ be a Cauchy sequence. Suppose that $\beta>0$ is a separation bound, so that $d(x,x^\prime) < \beta \iff x = x^\prime$. In particular, note that if one takes $\varepsilon < \beta$, then by the Cauchy property, there exists an $N \in \mathbb{N}$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \forall n ,m \geq N: d(x_n,x_m) < \varepsilon < \beta,
    \end{align*}
    but since $d(x_n,x_m) < \beta$ implies that $x_n = x_m$, it follows that $x_n \equiv x_N$ for all $n \geq N$, which constitutes the limit. Hence, any Cauchy sequence is eventually constant, and so $(X,d)$ is complete.

