        \xi &\mapsto \{n \in \mathbb{N} \mid \exists x \in X : (n,x) \in \xi\}.
    \end{align*}
    By Lemma~\ref{lem:helpful-labelled}, there is a finite set $I \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ such that 
    \begin{align*}
        k \geq N \implies \Pi(\xi_k) = I.
    \end{align*}
    Note that if $I$ is empty, then the claim is verified, as this immediately implies, by Lemma~\ref{lem:helpful-labelled}, that $k \geq N \implies\xi_k = \emptyset$. Therefore, we assume without loss of generality that $I \neq \emptyset$. For each $i \in I$, define 
    \begin{align*}
