    Thus $(x_k(i))_{k = N}^\infty$ is a Cauchy sequence for each $i \in I$. Since $X$ is assumed complete, each sequence $(x_k(i))_{k = N}^\infty$ has a limit, say $x(i) \in X$. We claim that 
    \begin{align*}
        \xi = \{(i,x(i)) \mid i \in I\}
    \end{align*}
    is the limit of the Cauchy sequence. For $k \geq N$ one has 
    \begin{align*}
        d_H^\ast(\xi_k, \xi) \leq \max_{i \in I} d(x_k(i), x(i)),
    \end{align*}
    since the same indexed point in $\xi$ is an admissible Hausdorff match for the corresponding indexed point in $\xi_k$, and conversely. The right-hand side corresponds to the maximum metric on a finite product of $X$. Therefore, one has that 
    \begin{align}
        \lim_{k\to\infty} d_H^\ast(\xi_k, \xi) = 0,
    \end{align}
    since $x_k(i) \to x(i)$ for each of the finitely many $i \in I$, which proves the claim.

results.statement:
\label{thm:labelled-properties}
    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space and endow $\mathbb{N}$ with the Euclidean distance. On $\mathbb{N}\times X$, define $d^\ast:(\mathbb{N}\times X) \times (\mathbb{N}\times X) \to [0, \infty]$ by 
    \begin{align*}
        d^\ast((n,x),(n^\prime, x^\prime)) = d(x,x^\prime) + |n-n^\prime|.
    \end{align*}
    Let $\mathscr{L}(X) \subseteq \mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N}\times X)$ be endowed with the Hausdorff distance $d_H^\ast : \mathscr{L}(X) \times \mathscr{L}(X) \to [0,\infty]$ induced by $d^\ast$; since $d^\ast$ is a metric on $\mathbb{N}\times X$, Theorem~\ref{thm:Hausdorff-finite-sets} makes $d_H^\ast$ a metric on $\mathscr{L}(X)$. It holds that
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item if $(X,d)$ is separable, then so is $(\mathscr{L}(X), d_H^\ast)$.\label{property:labelled-finite-separable}
        \item if $(X,d)$ is complete, then so is $(\mathscr{L}(X), d_H^\ast)$.\label{property:labelled-finite-complete}
    \end{enumerate}

steps.text:
For the first property, note that if either $\xi$ or $\eta$ is the empty set, then $d_H^\ast(\xi, \eta) < 1$ immediately implies $\xi = \eta = \emptyset$ as it is otherwise infinitely far from other elements by definition. Therefore, it suffices to show the properties for $\xi \neq \emptyset$ and $\eta \neq \emptyset$. Now let $r \in (0,1]$. Then
    \begin{align}
        d_H^\ast(\xi, \eta) < r
        &\implies \max_{(n,x)\in \xi}\min_{(n^\prime,x^\prime)\in \eta}
        d^\ast((n,x), (n^\prime, x^\prime)) \notag\\
        &\quad =\max_{(n,x)\in \xi}\min_{(n^\prime,x^\prime)\in \eta}
        \left(d(x,x^\prime) + |n-n^\prime|\right) < r.
        \label{eq:bound-labelled-1}
    \end{align}
    Consequently, the inequality is equivalent to
    \begin{align}\label{eq:labelled-conseq-1}
        \forall (n,x)\in \xi\ \exists(n^\prime, x^\prime) \in \eta : (d(x,x^\prime) < r) \land(n = n^\prime),
    \end{align}
    since $r \leq 1$ and $|n - n^\prime| < 1$ if and only if $n = n^\prime$. Similarly, 
    \begin{align}
        \max_{(n^\prime,x^\prime)\in \eta}\min_{(n,x)\in \xi}
        d^\ast((n,x), (n^\prime, x^\prime)) \notag\\
        \quad =\max_{(n^\prime,x^\prime)\in \eta}\min_{(n,x)\in \xi}
        \left(d(x,x^\prime) + |n-n^\prime|\right) < r
        \label{eq:bound-labelled-2}
    \end{align}
    if and only if
    \begin{align}\label{eq:labelled-conseq-2}
        \forall (n^\prime, x^\prime)\in \eta\ \exists(n,x) \in \xi : (d(x,x^\prime) < r) \land(n = n^\prime).
    \end{align}
    Note that by definition, when $d_H^\ast(\xi,\eta)<r$, then both \eqref{eq:bound-labelled-1} and \eqref{eq:bound-labelled-2} hold. For $r = 1$, \eqref{eq:labelled-conseq-1} and \eqref{eq:labelled-conseq-2} show that $\xi$ and $\eta$ use the same set of labels $n$. Since each label occurs at most once in an element of $\mathscr{L}(X)$, each of $\xi$ and $\eta$ has as many elements as its label set, and hence $|\xi| = |\eta|$. Moreover, \eqref{eq:labelled-conseq-1} is precisely the second property.

