results.statement:
\label{lem:helpful-labelled}
    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space and let $(\mathscr{L}(X), d_H^\ast)$ be the set of labelled finite sets with metric $d_H^\ast$ defined in Theorem~\ref{thm:labelled-properties}. Then, for every $\xi,\eta \in \mathscr{L}(X)$
    \begin{align*}
        d_H^\ast(\xi, \eta) < 1 \implies |\xi| = |\eta|.
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, for every $r \in (0,1]$,
    \begin{align*}
        d_H^\ast(\xi, \eta) < r \implies \forall (n,x) \in \xi\ \exists x^\prime \in X: (d(x,x^\prime) < r) \land (n, x^\prime) \in \eta.
    \end{align*}

\label{defn:tuple}\index{tuple space}
    Let $X$ be a non-empty set. The set 
    \begin{align}\label{defn:tuple-space}
        \mathscr{T}(X) = \bigcup_{n\in\mathbb{N}_0} X^n,
    \end{align}
    where $X^0$ is the singleton containing the empty function, is called the space of tuples over $X$, and the elements $\bm{x} \in \mathscr{T}(X)$ are called either $X$-tuples or simply tuples.

\label{defn:tuple-length}\index{tuple length}
    Let $X$ be a non-empty set and $\mathscr{T}(X)$ the space of tuples over $X$. Then, the mapping $\ell:\mathscr{T}(X)  \to \mathbb{N}_0$ defined by 
    \begin{align}
        \ell(\bm{x}) = |\mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})|, 
    \end{align}
    where $|\cdot|$ denotes the cardinality, is called the length map, and $\ell(\bm{x})$ is called the length of $\bm{x}$.

\label{defn:sorted-tuples}
    Let $(X,\preceq)$ be a partially ordered set and let $\prec$ denote the strict part of $\preceq$. A tuple $\bm{x} \in \mathscr{T}(X)$ is sorted with respect to $\preceq$ if 
    \begin{align*}
        \forall i,j \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}): i \leq j \implies \neg(\bm{x}(j) \prec \bm{x}(i)). 
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, the set of tuples that are sorted with respect to $\preceq$ is denoted by $\mathscr{S}(X, \preceq)$.

steps.text:
Suppose first that $\bm{x}$ is sorted. Then, by definition,
    \begin{align*}
        i \leq j \implies \neg(\bm{x}(j) \prec \bm{x}(i)).
    \end{align*}
    Since the order is total, for any $a,b \in X$ we have
    \begin{align*}
        \neg(b \prec a) \iff a \preceq b.
    \end{align*}
    Therefore,
    \begin{align*}
        i \leq j \implies \bm{x}(i) \preceq \bm{x}(j).
    \end{align*}

Conversely, if
    \begin{align*}
        i \leq j \implies \bm{x}(i) \preceq \bm{x}(j),
    \end{align*}
    then certainly
    \begin{align*}
        i \leq j \implies \neg(\bm{x}(j) \prec \bm{x}(i)),
    \end{align*}
    so $\bm{x}$ is sorted.

proof.text:
