results.statement:
\label{exmp:sort-discont}\index{sorting!discontinuity}
    Let $\mathbb{R}^2$ carry the Euclidean metric and let $\mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R}^2)$ carry the induced tuple Hausdorff metric, and let $\preceq$ be the lexicographic ordering on $\mathbb{R}^2$ defined by  
    \begin{align*}
        (x,y) \preceq (x^\prime, y^\prime) \iff x < x^\prime \lor ((x =x^\prime) \land (y \leq y^\prime)).
    \end{align*}
    The order is total and, as a result, the sorting map is unique. For $n\in\mathbb{N}$, define the sequence 
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{x}_n = i \mapsto \begin{cases}
            (0, 1) & i =1\\ 
            \left(\frac{1}{n}, 0\right) &i =2
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    and notice that $\bm{x}_n \in \mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}^2, \preceq)$ by construction. However, 
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{n \to \infty} \bm{x}_n = \bm{x}= i \mapsto \begin{cases}
            (0, 1) & i = 1 \\ 
            (0, 0) & i = 2,
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    but clearly $\bm{x}\notin\mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}^2,\preceq)$. Moreover, since $\varsigma$ acts as the identity on $\mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}^2, \preceq)$, it follows that
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{x} =\lim_{n\to\infty} \bm{x}_n =\lim_{n\to\infty} \varsigma(\bm{x}_n) \neq \varsigma(\lim_{n\to \infty}\bm{x}_n) = \varsigma(\bm{x}) =i \mapsto\begin{cases}
            (0,0) &i = 1\\ 
            (0, 1) & i =2.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}

