steps.text:
For each $i,j \in \{1,\dots,n\}$ define
    \begin{align*}
        G_{i,j}
        &= \{\bm{x} \in X^n \mid i \overset{\bm{x}}{\leftrightsquigarrow} j\}\\
        &= \{\bm{x} \in X^n \mid \bm{x}(i) \preceq \bm{x}(j) \lor \bm{x}(j) \preceq \bm{x}(i)\}.
    \end{align*}
    Each set $G_{i,j}$ is measurable because the order relation $\preceq \subseteq X \times X$ is Borel and the coordinate evaluation maps on $X^n$ are continuous.

Next, define
    \begin{align*}
        \overline{G}_{i,j} = \{\bm{x} \in X^n \mid i \overset{\bm{x}}{\sim} j\}.
    \end{align*}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:transitive-closure-finite}, two indices are connected if and only if they can be joined by a path $i = i_0, i_1, \dots, i_k = j$ in $\{1,\dots,n\}$ of length $k \leq n-1$. Hence, with the convention that the empty intersection (for $k=0$) equals $X^n$,
    \begin{align*}
        \overline{G}_{i,j}
        = \bigcup_{k=0}^{n-1}\, \bigcup_{\substack{i_0,\dots,i_k \in \{1,\dots,n\}\\ i_0 = i,\ i_k = j}}\, \bigcap_{l=0}^{k-1} G_{i_l,i_{l+1}},
    \end{align*}
    a finite union of finite intersections. Therefore, each $\overline{G}_{i,j}$ is measurable.

For each partition $P$ of $\{1,\dots,n\}$ define
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{E}(P)
        = \bigcap_{C \in P}\, \bigcap_{i,j \in C} \overline{G}_{i,j}
        \cap
        \bigcap_{\substack{C,C^\prime \in P\\ C \neq C^\prime}}\, \bigcap_{i \in C,\, j \in C^\prime} \overline{G}_{i,j}^{\mathtt{C}}.
    \end{align*}
    Thus $\mathscr{E}(P)$ is exactly the set of tuples whose connectivity partition of $\{1,\dots,n\}$ equals $P$. Since $\overset{\bm{x}}{\sim}$ is an equivalence relation, the sets $\mathscr{E}(P)$, with $P$ ranging over the finitely many partitions of $\{1,\dots,n\}$, form a finite measurable partition of $X^n$.

Now, the diagonal $\Delta_X = \{(x,x) \mid x \in X\} \subseteq X \times X$ is Borel because $X$ is metric. Hence the strict order
    \begin{align*}
        \prec = \preceq \setminus \Delta_X
    \end{align*}
    is Borel as well. For $i,j \in \{1,\dots,n\}$ define
    \begin{align*}
        A_{i,j} = \{\bm{x} \in X^n \mid \bm{x}(j) \prec \bm{x}(i)\}.
    \end{align*}
    Each $A_{i,j}$ is measurable.

Fix a non-empty set $C \subseteq \{1,\dots,n\}$ and a bijection $\pi:\{1,\dots,|C|\} \to C$. Define
    \begin{align*}
        C^{(1)} &= C,\\
        C^{(r)} &= C \setminus \{\pi(1),\dots,\pi(r-1)\}, \qquad r > 1.
    \end{align*}
    For each $r \in \{1,\dots,|C|\}$ set
    \begin{align*}
        M_{C,\pi,r}
        =
        \bigcap_{j \in C^{(r)}} A_{\pi(r),j}^{\mathtt{C}}
        \cap
        \bigcap_{\substack{i \in C^{(r)}\\ i < \pi(r)}} \bigcup_{j \in C^{(r)}} A_{i,j}.
    \end{align*}
    The first intersection states that no remaining entry is strictly smaller than the entry at $\pi(r)$, so $\bm{x}(\pi(r))$ is minimal among the remaining entries. The second states that every remaining index $i$ smaller than $\pi(r)$ carries an entry that fails to be minimal, so among the indices carrying minimal remaining entries the least one is exactly $\pi(r)$. Therefore
    \begin{align*}
        B_{C,\pi} = \bigcap_{r=1}^{|C|} M_{C,\pi,r}
    \end{align*}
    is precisely the set of tuples for which the minimum sorting permutation on the block $C$ equals $\pi$: the minimum sorting permutation on $C$ is defined by exactly this greedy recursion, selecting at step $r$ the least index among those carrying a $\preceq$-minimal remaining entry, so by induction on $r$ the two descriptions agree, and distinct permutations $\pi$ give disjoint sets $B_{C,\pi}$. In particular, $B_{C,\pi}$ is measurable.

Now fix a partition $P = \{C_1,\dots,C_m\}$ of $\{1,\dots,n\}$, ordered so that
    \begin{align*}
        \min C_1 < \dots < \min C_m,
    \end{align*}
    and choose bijections $\pi_k:\{1,\dots,|C_k|\} \to C_k$ for $k=1,\dots,m$. Define
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{E}(P,\pi_1,\dots,\pi_m)
        = \mathscr{E}(P) \cap \bigcap_{k=1}^m B_{C_k,\pi_k}.
    \end{align*}
    As $P$ and the $\pi_k$ vary, these sets form a finite measurable partition of $X^n$.

On each set $\mathscr{E}(P,\pi_1,\dots,\pi_m)$, both the comparability components and the minimum sorting permutation within each component are fixed. Since $C_1,\dots,C_m$ partition $\{1,\dots,n\}$, we have $\pi_1\oplus\dots\oplus\pi_m \in \mathscr{P}_n$. Consequently, on this set the map $\varsigma_{\mathrm{min}}$ agrees with the fixed permutation map
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{x} \mapsto \bm{x}[\pi_1 \oplus \dots \oplus \pi_m].
    \end{align*}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:permuting-continuous}, this map is continuous, hence measurable. Therefore, by Theorem~\ref{thm:measurable-partition}, the restriction of $\varsigma_{\mathrm{min}}$ to $X^n$ is measurable.

proof.text:
By the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery}, it suffices to show that the restriction of $\varsigma_{\mathrm{min}}$ to each $X^n$ is Borel measurable when $X^n$ is endowed with the maximum product metric. The cases $n = 0$ and $n = 1$ are trivial, since $\varsigma_{\mathrm{min}}$ is then the identity. Therefore, fix $n > 1$.

    For each $i,j \in \{1,\dots,n\}$ define
