    is Borel as well. For $i,j \in \{1,\dots,n\}$ define
    \begin{align*}
        A_{i,j} = \{\bm{x} \in X^n \mid \bm{x}(j) \prec \bm{x}(i)\}.
    \end{align*}
    Each $A_{i,j}$ is measurable.

    Fix a non-empty set $C \subseteq \{1,\dots,n\}$ and a bijection $\pi:\{1,\dots,|C|\} \to C$. Define
    \begin{align*}
        C^{(1)} &= C,\\
        C^{(r)} &= C \setminus \{\pi(1),\dots,\pi(r-1)\}, \qquad r > 1.
    \end{align*}
    For each $r \in \{1,\dots,|C|\}$ set
    \begin{align*}
        M_{C,\pi,r}
        =
        \bigcap_{j \in C^{(r)}} A_{\pi(r),j}^{\mathtt{C}}
        \cap
        \bigcap_{\substack{i \in C^{(r)}\\ i < \pi(r)}} \bigcup_{j \in C^{(r)}} A_{i,j}.
    \end{align*}
    The first intersection states that no remaining entry is strictly smaller than the entry at $\pi(r)$, so $\bm{x}(\pi(r))$ is minimal among the remaining entries. The second states that every remaining index $i$ smaller than $\pi(r)$ carries an entry that fails to be minimal, so among the indices carrying minimal remaining entries the least one is exactly $\pi(r)$. Therefore
    \begin{align*}
