proof.text:
To prove \eqref{property:identifier-cancel-continuous}, we must show that for every $(\bm{x}_0, J_0) \in \mathscr{T}(X) \times \mathscr{F}(I)$ and every $\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a $\delta > 0$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \max\bigl(\mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x}, \bm{x}_0), d_H(J, J_0)\bigr) < \delta \implies \mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x} \ominus_I J, \bm{x}_0 \ominus_I J_0) < \varepsilon.
    \end{align*}
    Fix $\varepsilon > 0$ and choose $\delta \in (0, \min(1, \varepsilon))$. Suppose that $\max(\mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x},\bm{x}_0),d_H(J,J_0))<\delta < 1$. By the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery} (Theorem~\ref{thm:the-standard-machinery}), we have $\ell(\bm{x})=\ell(\bm{x}_0)$ and, whenever $\ell(\bm{x}_0) > 0$,
    \begin{align*}
        d(\bm{x}(k),\bm{x}_0(k)) < \delta < 1 \qquad \forall k \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0).
    \end{align*}
    By the definition of $d$ and $d_I$, the inequality $d(\bm{x}(k),\bm{x}_0(k)) < 1$ implies $\mathrm{id}(\bm{x}(k))=\mathrm{id}(\bm{x}_0(k))$ for every $k \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0)$. Furthermore, since $(I,d_I)$ is uniformly discrete with bound $1$, so is $(\mathscr{F}(I),d_H)$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-hausdorff-uniformly-discrete}, whence $d_H(J, J_0) < 1$ implies $J=J_0$. Consequently, the index sets of matching identifiers coincide:
    \begin{align*}
        \eta_J(\bm{x}) &= \{k \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}) \mid \mathrm{id}(\bm{x}(k)) \in J\}\\
        &= \{k \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0) \mid \mathrm{id}(\bm{x}_0(k)) \in J_0\} = \eta_{J_0}(\bm{x}_0).
    \end{align*}
    On this neighbourhood, we therefore have $\bm{x}\ominus_I J=\bm{x}\ominus\eta_{J_0}(\bm{x}_0)$ and $\bm{x}_0 \ominus_I J_0 = \bm{x}_0 \ominus \eta_{J_0}(\bm{x}_0)$, so the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:analytic-properties-actions}\eqref{property:cancel-continuous} yields
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x} \ominus_I J, \bm{x}_0 \ominus_I J_0) = \mathscr{d}_H\bigl(\bm{x} \ominus \eta_{J_0}(\bm{x}_0), \bm{x}_0 \ominus \eta_{J_0}(\bm{x}_0)\bigr) < \delta < \varepsilon,
    \end{align*}
    which proves that $\ominus_I$ is continuous.

    Next, we prove \eqref{property:identifier-distinct-clopen}. By the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery}, each fixed-length layer $X^n$ is clopen in $\mathscr{T}(X)$. For $n \in \{0, 1\}$, we have $\mathscr{T}_{\neq}(X) \cap X^n = X^n$, which is clopen. For $n \geq 2$, the intersection of $\mathscr{T}_{\neq}(X)$ with $X^n$ is given by
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{T}_{\neq}(X) \cap X^n = \bigcap_{1 \leq k < l \leq n} \bigl\{\bm{x} \in X^n \bigm| \mathrm{id}(\bm{x}(k)) \neq \mathrm{id}(\bm{x}(l))\bigr\}.
    \end{align*}
    Because $\mathrm{id}:X \to (I, d_I)$ is locally constant, each comparison set in the intersection is clopen in $X^n$, so $\mathscr{T}_{\neq}(X) \cap X^n$ is clopen in $X^n$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}_0$, and hence $\mathscr{T}_{\neq}(X)$ is clopen in $\mathscr{T}(X)$. By the same argument on the clopen product layers $X^n \times X^m$, the set of pairs
    \begin{align*}
        \bigl\{(\bm{x}, \bm{y}) \in \mathscr{T}_{\neq}(X)^2 \bigm| \forall k \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})\, \forall l \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{y}) : \mathrm{id}(\bm{x}(k)) \neq \mathrm{id}(\bm{y}(l))\bigr\}
    \end{align*}
    is clopen in $\mathscr{T}_{\neq}(X)^2$, and concatenating any such pair via $\oplus$ yields a tuple with pairwise distinct identifiers, hence in $\mathscr{T}_{\neq}(X)$. Similarly, index-based cancellation $\ominus$ and identifier-based cancellation $\ominus_I$ compose $\mathrm{id} \circ \bm{x}$ with strictly increasing reindexing maps, and any sorting map $\varsigma$ composes $\mathrm{id} \circ \bm{x}$ with a bijection $\pi \in \mathscr{P}_{\ell(\bm{x})}$, so all three preserve injectivity of $\mathrm{id} \circ \bm{x}$. Finally, if $Y$ is Polish, then $X = Y \times I$ is Polish, so $\mathscr{T}(X)$ is Polish by Theorem~\ref{thm:tuple-space-properties}, and $\mathscr{T}_{\neq}(X)$, being a closed (and open) subset of a Polish space, is Polish.

    To prove \eqref{property:identifier-cancel-measurable}, note that since $Y$ is separable and $I$ is countable, both $X$ and $\mathscr{T}(X)$ are separable (Theorem~\ref{thm:tuple-space-properties}), while $\mathscr{F}(I)$ is countable and discrete. Thus the product Borel $\sigma$-algebra $\mathcal{B}(\mathscr{T}(X)) \otimes \mathcal{B}(\mathscr{F}(I))$ coincides with $\mathcal{B}(\mathscr{T}(X) \times \mathscr{F}(I))$. Since $\ominus_I$ is continuous by \eqref{property:identifier-cancel-continuous} and $\varsigma$ is Borel measurable by assumption, their composition $(\bm{x}, J) \mapsto \varsigma(\bm{x} \ominus_I J)$ is Borel measurable.

    Finally, to prove \eqref{property:identifier-forgetful}, we must show that $\varpi_* : \mathscr{T}(X) \to \mathscr{T}(Y)$ is continuous. Fix $\bm{x}_0 \in \mathscr{T}(X)$ and $\varepsilon > 0$, and choose $\delta \in (0, \min(1, \varepsilon))$. If $\mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x}, \bm{x}_0) < \delta < 1$, then $\ell(\bm{x}) = \ell(\bm{x}_0)$, and since $\varpi_*$ preserves tuple lengths, $\ell(\varpi_*(\bm{x})) = \ell(\varpi_*(\bm{x}_0))$. For $\ell(\bm{x}_0) = 0$, both images equal $\bm{e}$; for $\ell(\bm{x}_0) > 0$, the $1$-Lipschitz bound $d_Y(\varpi(x), \varpi(x^\prime)) \leq d(x, x^\prime)$ yields
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{d}_H\bigl(\varpi_*(\bm{x}), \varpi_*(\bm{x}_0)\bigr)
        &= \max_{k \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0)} d_Y\bigl(\varpi(\bm{x}(k)), \varpi(\bm{x}_0(k))\bigr)\\
        &\leq \max_{k \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0)} d\bigl(\bm{x}(k), \bm{x}_0(k)\bigr)
        < \delta < \varepsilon.
    \end{align*}
    Thus $\varpi_*$ is continuous, and composing any continuous or Borel measurable map $g$ on $\mathscr{T}(Y)$ with $\varpi_*$ preserves continuity or Borel measurability, as desired.

results.statement:
\label{prop:identifier-cancel-continuous}\index{continuity!identifier-based cancellation}
    Let $(Y,d_Y)$ be a metric space, let $(I,\leq)$ be a countable, totally ordered set, and let $X=Y\times I$ be endowed with the metric
    \begin{align*}
        d\bigl((y,i),(y^\prime,j)\bigr)=\max\bigl(d_Y(y,y^\prime),d_I(i,j)\bigr),
        \qquad
        d_I(i,j)=\mathbbm{1}(i\neq j),
    \end{align*}
    and with the projections $\mathrm{id}(y,i)=i$ and $\varpi(y,i)=y$. Moreover, let
    \begin{enumerate}[-]
        \item $(\mathscr{T}(X),\mathscr{d}_H)$ and $(\mathscr{T}(Y),\mathscr{d}_H)$ be the tuple spaces over $X$ and $Y$, endowed with the tuple Hausdorff distances,
        \item $\mathscr{T}_{\neq}(X)=\{\bm{x}\in\mathscr{T}(X)\mid\mathrm{id}\circ\bm{x}\text{ is injective}\}$ be the set of tuples with distinct identifiers, and
        \item $(\mathscr{F}(I),d_H)$ be the set of finite subsets of $I$, endowed with the Hausdorff distance induced by $d_I$.
    \end{enumerate}
    If all product spaces are endowed with the product metrics, then
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item $\ominus_I:\mathscr{T}(X)\times\mathscr{F}(I)\to\mathscr{T}(X)$ as defined in Definition~\ref{defn:identifier-cancel} is continuous,\label{property:identifier-cancel-continuous}
        \item $\mathscr{T}_{\neq}(X)$ is clopen in $\mathscr{T}(X)$, Polish whenever $Y$ is, and closed under $\ominus$, $\ominus_I$, and every sorting map; moreover, the pairs in $\mathscr{T}_{\neq}(X)^2$ whose identifier sets are disjoint form a clopen set, which $\oplus$ maps into $\mathscr{T}_{\neq}(X)$,\label{property:identifier-distinct-clopen}
        \item if $Y$ is separable, $\preceq$ is a Borel partial order on $X$, such as $\preceq_I$ under the assumptions of Proposition~\ref{prop:identifier-tie-breaking-borel}, and $\varsigma:\mathscr{T}(X)\to\mathscr{S}(X,\preceq)$ is a measurable sorting map, where $\mathscr{S}(X,\preceq)$ carries its subspace Borel $\sigma$-algebra, then $(\bm{x},J)\mapsto\varsigma(\bm{x}\ominus_I J)$ is measurable, and\label{property:identifier-cancel-measurable}
        \item $\varpi_*:\mathscr{T}(X)\to\mathscr{T}(Y)$, $\bm{x}\mapsto\varpi\circ\bm{x}$, is continuous, so a map of the form $g\circ\varpi_*$ is continuous or Borel measurable whenever $g$ is.\label{property:identifier-forgetful}
    \end{enumerate}

\label{defn:actions}\index{action space}\index{application map}
    Let $X$ be a non-empty set and $\mathscr{T}(X)$ the associated tuple space. Let $A=\{\oplus, \ominus, \circledast\}$ denote the set containing the concatenation, cancellation, and modification operations as in Definitions~\ref{defn:concat-tuple}, \ref{defn:cancel}, and \ref{defn:modification}, respectively. Let 
    \begin{align*}
        F_\alpha = \begin{cases}
            \mathscr{T}(X) & \alpha = \oplus\\
            \mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N}) & \alpha = \ominus \\ 
            \mathscr{L}(X) &\alpha = \circledast.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    Then we call the set $\mathscr{A}(X) = \bigsqcup_{\alpha \in A} F_\alpha$ the action space. Moreover, we define the mapping $\lhd : \mathscr{T}(X) \times  \mathscr{A}(X) \to \mathscr{T}(X)$ by
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{x} \lhd (o, \alpha) = \alpha(\bm{x}, o),
    \end{align*}
    where $\bm{x} \in \mathscr{T}(X)$ and $(o,\alpha) \in \mathscr{A}(X)$. We call $\lhd$ the application map.

\label{defn:composite-actions}
    Let $X$ be a non-empty set, and let $\mathscr{A}(X)$ be the associated action space. Then, we call 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{C}(X) = \mathscr{T}(\mathscr{A}(X))
    \end{align*}
    the set of composite actions. Moreover, the application map, $\lhd:\mathscr{T}(X) \times \mathscr{A}(X) \to \mathscr{T}(X)$, extends to a mapping $\lhd :\mathscr{T}(X) \times \mathscr{C}(X) \to \mathscr{T}(X)$ defined by  
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{x} \lhd \bm{c} = \begin{cases}
            \bm{x} &\bm{c}=\bm{e}\\ 
            \bm{x} \lhd \bm{c}(1) \lhd \dots \lhd \bm{c}(n) & \ell(\bm{c}) = n > 0,
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    where $\lhd$ is evaluated from left to right, i.e.\ $\bm{x} \lhd \bm{c}(1) \lhd \dots \lhd \bm{c}(n)=(\cdots((\bm{x} \lhd \bm{c}(1)) \lhd \bm{c}(2))\cdots) \lhd \bm{c}(n)$.

\label{cor:action-metrisation}
    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space, and let
    \begin{enumerate}[-]
        \item $(\mathscr{T}(X),\mathscr{d}_H)$ be the tuple space over $X$ endowed with the tuple Hausdorff distance (Theorem~\ref{thm:tuple-space-metrisation}),
        \item $(\mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N}),d_H)$ be the set of finite subsets of the natural numbers, endowed with the Hausdorff distance induced by the Euclidean distance (Definition~\ref{defn:Hausdorff-distance}), and
        \item $(\mathscr{L}(X),d_H^\ast)$ be the set of labelled finite sets over $X$, endowed with the index-augmented Hausdorff distance (Theorem~\ref{thm:labelled-properties}).
    \end{enumerate}
    Let $A = \{\oplus, \ominus, \circledast\}$ be the set of operations defined in Definitions~\ref{defn:concat-tuple}, \ref{defn:cancel}, and \ref{defn:modification}, respectively. Let
    \begin{align*}
        F_\alpha = \begin{cases}
            \mathscr{T}(X) & \alpha = \oplus\\
            \mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N}) & \alpha = \ominus \\ 
            \mathscr{L}(X) &\alpha = \circledast
        \end{cases},\quad\quad d_\alpha = \begin{cases}
            \mathscr{d}_H & \alpha = \oplus\\
            d_H & \alpha = \ominus \\ 
            d_H^\ast &\alpha = \circledast
        \end{cases},
    \end{align*}
    and let $\mathscr{A}(X) = \bigsqcup_{\alpha \in A} F_\alpha$. Define $\Delta : \mathscr{A}(X)\times \mathscr{A}(X) \to [0,\infty]$ by 
    \begin{align*}
        \Delta((o,\alpha),(o^\prime,\alpha^\prime))=\begin{cases}
            d_\alpha(o,o^\prime)&\alpha = \alpha^\prime \\
            \infty &\alpha \neq \alpha^\prime.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    Then $(\mathscr{A}(X), \Delta)$ is a metric space. Moreover, if $(X,d)$ is complete, then so is $(\mathscr{A}(X), \Delta)$. Likewise, if $(X,d)$ is separable, then so is $(\mathscr{A}(X), \Delta)$.

\label{prop:application-continuous}\index{application map!continuity}
    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space, $(\mathscr{T}(X), \mathscr{d}_H)$ be the tuple space endowed with the tuple Hausdorff distance, and let $(\mathscr{A}(X), \Delta)$ be the action space with the metric as defined in Corollary~\ref{cor:action-metrisation}. Let $\lhd:\mathscr{T}(X)\times \mathscr{A}(X) \to \mathscr{T}(X)$ be the application map defined by 
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{x} \lhd (o,\alpha) = \alpha(\bm{x}, o).
    \end{align*}
    Then $\lhd$ is continuous.

\label{thm:composite-action-metric}
    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space and let $(\mathscr{A}(X), \Delta)$ denote the action space endowed with the metric as in Corollary~\ref{cor:action-metrisation}. Let 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{C}(X)=\mathscr{T}(\mathscr{A}(X))
    \end{align*}
    denote the set of composite actions, and let $\Delta_H :\mathscr{C}(X)\times \mathscr{C}(X) \to [0,\infty]$ be the tuple Hausdorff distance induced by $\Delta$. Then, $(\mathscr{C}(X), \Delta_H)$ is a metric space. Moreover, if $(X,d)$ is complete, then so is $(\mathscr{C}(X),\Delta_H)$. Likewise, if $(X,d)$ is separable, then so is $(\mathscr{C}(X), \Delta_H)$.

steps.text:
For each $n \in \mathbb{N}_0$, let
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{C}_n(X) = \{\bm{c} \in \mathscr{C}(X) \mid \ell(\bm{c}) = n\}.
    \end{align*}
    By the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery} (Theorem~\ref{thm:the-standard-machinery}), each layer $\mathscr{C}_n(X)$ is homeomorphic to the finite product $\mathscr{A}(X)^n$ endowed with the maximum product metric.

Define maps
    \begin{align*}
        \Phi_0 &: \mathscr{T}(X) \to \mathscr{T}(X), \\ 
        \Phi_n &: \mathscr{T}(X) \times \mathscr{A}(X)^n \to \mathscr{T}(X)
    \end{align*}
    recursively by
    \begin{align*}
        \Phi_0(\bm{x}) &= \bm{x},\\
        \Phi_n(\bm{x}, a_1,\dots,a_n) &= \Phi_{n-1}(\bm{x}, a_1,\dots,a_{n-1}) \lhd a_n
    \end{align*}
    for $n \geq 1$. The map $\Phi_0$ is continuous, and if $\Phi_{n-1}$ is continuous, then $\Phi_n$ is continuous by Proposition~\ref{prop:application-continuous}. Hence $\Phi_n$ is continuous for every $n$.

Under the identification of $\mathscr{C}_n(X)$ with $\mathscr{A}(X)^n$, the restriction of the extended application map
    \begin{align*}
        \lhd : \mathscr{T}(X)\times \mathscr{C}(X) \to \mathscr{T}(X)
    \end{align*}
    to $\mathscr{T}(X)\times \mathscr{C}_n(X)$ is exactly $\Phi_n$, by \eqref{eq:recursive-application}; for $n=0$, this uses the identification of $\mathscr{T}(X)\times\mathscr{C}_0(X)=\mathscr{T}(X)\times\{\bm{e}\}$ with $\mathscr{T}(X)$. Therefore, the restriction to each fixed-length layer is continuous.

Finally, the composite-action space is the disjoint union of its length layers,
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{C}(X) = \bigsqcup_{n\in\mathbb{N}_0} \mathscr{C}_n(X),
    \end{align*}
    and the tuple metric is topologically equivalent to the corresponding disjoint-union metric by the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery} (Theorem~\ref{thm:the-standard-machinery}). Taking the product with $\mathscr{T}(X)$ distributes over this disjoint union, with each product layer open. The continuity of the global map therefore follows from the pasting lemma for disjoint unions (Theorem~\ref{thm:pasting-lemma}).

