results.statement:
\label{prop:tuple-projection-continuous}
     Let $(X_\alpha, d_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ be a finite non-empty family of metric spaces, $X = \prod_{\alpha\in A} X_\alpha$ and let $d: X \times X \to [0,\infty]$ be the maximum product metric on $X$. Let $(\mathscr{T}(X_\alpha), \mathscr{d}_{H,\alpha})_{\alpha \in A}$ be the associated tuple Hausdorff spaces, and let $(\mathscr{T}(X), \mathscr{d}_H)$ be the tuple Hausdorff space associated with $(X,d)$. Then
     \begin{enumerate}
         \item the projection $\bm{P}_\alpha :\mathscr{T}(X) \to \mathscr{T}(X_\alpha)$ is continuous for every $\alpha \in A$.
         \item the tuple decomposition $\bm{P}:\mathscr{T}(X) \to \prod_{\alpha \in A}\mathscr{T}(X_\alpha)$ is continuous whenever $\prod_{\alpha \in A}\mathscr{T}(X_\alpha)$ is endowed with the product topology.
         \item the mapping $\bm{P}$ is injective and hence invertible on its image.
     \end{enumerate}

\label{defn:separation-operators}\index{separation operators}
    Let $(X_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ be a family of non-empty sets, $X = \bigsqcup_{\alpha\in A} X_\alpha$. For each $\alpha \in A$, define $\bm{\Pi}_\alpha : \mathscr{T}(X) \to \mathscr{T}(X_\alpha)$ by 
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x} = \bm{P}_{\mathrm{element}} \left(\bm{x}[\{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}) ~\mid~ \bm{x}(i) \in X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}\}]\right).
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, define the mapping $\bm{\Pi}: \mathscr{T}(X) \to \prod_{\alpha \in A}\mathscr{T}(X_\alpha)$ by 
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{\Pi}\bm{x} = (\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x})_{\alpha \in A}. 
    \end{align*}
    We call each $\bm{\Pi}_\alpha$ the $\alpha$-separation operator, and $\bm{\Pi}$ the separation operator.

\label{rem:separation-element-projection}
    Note that we apply the projection $\bm{P}_{\mathrm{element}}$ because the disjoint union space has elements of the form $(x, \alpha)$ where $\alpha \in A$ and $x \in X_\alpha$. However, when we separate the elements into the sub-tuple containing only elements from $X_\alpha$, there is no need to carry the $\alpha$ into this space. In other words, 
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{x}\left[\{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}) ~\mid~ \bm{x}(i) \in X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}\}\right] \in \mathscr{T}(X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}), 
    \end{align*}
    but then projecting onto the first component yields
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{P}_{\mathrm{element}}\left(\bm{x}\left[\{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}) ~\mid~ \bm{x}(i) \in X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}\}\right]\right) \in \mathscr{T}(X_\alpha),
    \end{align*}
    as desired.

steps.text:
To prove \eqref{prop:separation-1}, we must show that for any $\bm{x}_0 \in \mathscr{T}(X)$ and $\varepsilon >0$ there exists a $\delta > 0 $ such that 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x}, \bm{x}_0) < \delta \implies \mathscr{d}_{H,\alpha}(\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x}, \bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x}_0) < \varepsilon.
    \end{align*}
    Choose $0<\delta < \min(1,\varepsilon)$. Assume, without loss of generality, that $\bm{x}_0 \neq \bm{e}$; otherwise, the proof is trivial. There are two cases. First, if $\forall i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0):\bm{x}_0(i) \notin X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}$, then choosing $\delta$ as above yields the desired result, as $\bm{x} \in B_{\delta}(\bm{x}_0)$ must have $\forall i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}):\bm{x}(i) \notin X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}$, and hence $\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x}_0 = \bm{e} = \bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x}$. In the second case, the set
    \begin{align*}
        I=\{i\in\mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0)\mid \bm{x}_0(i)\in X_\alpha\times\{\alpha\}\}
    \end{align*}
    is non-empty.

For the second property, we once again let $\bm{x}_0 \in \mathscr{T}(X)$ and $\varepsilon > 0$ be given. If $\bm{x}_0=\bm{e}$, then choosing $0<\delta<\min(1,\varepsilon)$ gives $\bm{x}=\bm{e}$ whenever $\mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x},\bm{x}_0)<\delta$ by the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery}. Hence $\bm{\Pi}_\alpha\bm{x}=\bm{\Pi}_\alpha\bm{x}_0=\bm{e}$ for every $\alpha\in A$, so the sup product distance is $0<\varepsilon$. We therefore assume $\bm{x}_0\neq\bm{e}$ below. Now, for the given $\bm{x}_0$ define 
    \begin{align*}
        A(\bm{x}_0) = \{\alpha \in A \mid \exists i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0): \bm{x}_0(i) \in X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}\},
    \end{align*}
    and note that by choosing $0<\delta < \min(1, \varepsilon)$ one has that for $\bm{x}\in B_\delta(\bm{x}_0)$ 
    \begin{align*}
        A(\bm{x}) = A(\bm{x}_0).
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, note that $|A(\bm{x}_0)| < \infty$ since $\bm{x}_0$ is a tuple.
    It now follows that
    \begin{align*}
        \varepsilon > \delta > \mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x}, \bm{x}_0) &= \max_{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0)} d(\bm{x}(i), \bm{x}_0(i)) \\
        &= \max_{\alpha \in A(\bm{x}_0)} \max_{\substack{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0)\\
        \bm{x}_0(i) \in X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}}} d_\alpha(\bm{P}_{\mathrm{element}}(\bm{x}(i)), \bm{P}_{\mathrm{element}}(\bm{x}_0(i)))\\
        &= \max_{\alpha \in A(\bm{x}_0)} \mathscr{d}_{H,\alpha}(\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x}, \bm{\Pi}_\alpha\bm{x}_0) \\
        &= \sup_{\alpha \in A} \mathscr{d}_{H,\alpha}(\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x}, \bm{\Pi}_\alpha\bm{x}_0),
    \end{align*}
    where the latter is by definition the sup product metric over $A$, and the supremum is attained over the finite active set because the projections $\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x} = \bm{\Pi}_{\alpha} \bm{x}_0 = \bm{e}$ for all $\alpha \in A \setminus A(\bm{x}_0)$. Finally, since 
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{\Pi} \bm{x} = (\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x})_{\alpha \in A},
    \end{align*}
    the result follows.

proof.text:
To prove \eqref{prop:separation-1}, we must show that for any $\bm{x}_0 \in \mathscr{T}(X)$ and $\varepsilon >0$ there exists a $\delta > 0 $ such that 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x}, \bm{x}_0) < \delta \implies \mathscr{d}_{H,\alpha}(\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x}, \bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x}_0) < \varepsilon.
    \end{align*}
    Choose $0<\delta < \min(1,\varepsilon)$. Assume, without loss of generality, that $\bm{x}_0 \neq \bm{e}$; otherwise, the proof is trivial. There are two cases. First, if $\forall i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0):\bm{x}_0(i) \notin X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}$, then choosing $\delta$ as above yields the desired result, as $\bm{x} \in B_{\delta}(\bm{x}_0)$ must have $\forall i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}):\bm{x}(i) \notin X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}$, and hence $\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x}_0 = \bm{e} = \bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x}$. In the second case, the set
    \begin{align*}
        I=\{i\in\mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0)\mid \bm{x}_0(i)\in X_\alpha\times\{\alpha\}\}
    \end{align*}
    is non-empty.

    Now, choose $\bm{x} \in B_\delta (\bm{x}_0)$ and note that one has $\forall i \in I : \bm{x}(i) \in X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}$, and hence
    \begin{align*}
        \varepsilon > \delta > \mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x}, \bm{x}_0) &= \max_{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0)} d(\bm{x}(i), \bm{x}_0(i)) \\
        &\geq \max_{i \in I} d(\bm{x}(i), \bm{x}_0(i)) \\
        &=\max_{i \in I} d_\alpha(\bm{P}_{\mathrm{element}} \bm{x}(i), \bm{P}_{\mathrm{element}} \bm{x}_0(i))\\
        &= \max_{j \in \{1, \dots, |I|\}} d_\alpha(\bm{P}_{\mathrm{element}}\left(\bm{x}[I]\right)(j), \bm{P}_{\mathrm{element}} \left(\bm{x}_0[I]\right)(j))\\
        &= \mathscr{d}_{H,\alpha}(\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x}, \bm{\Pi}_\alpha\bm{x}_0)
    \end{align*}
    as desired, since $I = \{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0) \mid \bm{x}_0(i) \in X_\alpha\times \{\alpha\}\} = \{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0) \mid \bm{x}(i) \in X_\alpha\times \{\alpha\}\}$. 

    For the second property, we once again let $\bm{x}_0 \in \mathscr{T}(X)$ and $\varepsilon > 0$ be given. If $\bm{x}_0=\bm{e}$, then choosing $0<\delta<\min(1,\varepsilon)$ gives $\bm{x}=\bm{e}$ whenever $\mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x},\bm{x}_0)<\delta$ by the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery}. Hence $\bm{\Pi}_\alpha\bm{x}=\bm{\Pi}_\alpha\bm{x}_0=\bm{e}$ for every $\alpha\in A$, so the sup product distance is $0<\varepsilon$. We therefore assume $\bm{x}_0\neq\bm{e}$ below. Now, for the given $\bm{x}_0$ define 
    \begin{align*}
        A(\bm{x}_0) = \{\alpha \in A \mid \exists i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0): \bm{x}_0(i) \in X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}\},
    \end{align*}
    and note that by choosing $0<\delta < \min(1, \varepsilon)$ one has that for $\bm{x}\in B_\delta(\bm{x}_0)$ 
    \begin{align*}
        A(\bm{x}) = A(\bm{x}_0).
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, note that $|A(\bm{x}_0)| < \infty$ since $\bm{x}_0$ is a tuple.
    It now follows that
    \begin{align*}
        \varepsilon > \delta > \mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x}, \bm{x}_0) &= \max_{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0)} d(\bm{x}(i), \bm{x}_0(i)) \\
        &= \max_{\alpha \in A(\bm{x}_0)} \max_{\substack{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}_0)\\
        \bm{x}_0(i) \in X_\alpha \times \{\alpha\}}} d_\alpha(\bm{P}_{\mathrm{element}}(\bm{x}(i)), \bm{P}_{\mathrm{element}}(\bm{x}_0(i)))\\
        &= \max_{\alpha \in A(\bm{x}_0)} \mathscr{d}_{H,\alpha}(\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x}, \bm{\Pi}_\alpha\bm{x}_0) \\
        &= \sup_{\alpha \in A} \mathscr{d}_{H,\alpha}(\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x}, \bm{\Pi}_\alpha\bm{x}_0),
    \end{align*}
    where the latter is by definition the sup product metric over $A$, and the supremum is attained over the finite active set because the projections $\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x} = \bm{\Pi}_{\alpha} \bm{x}_0 = \bm{e}$ for all $\alpha \in A \setminus A(\bm{x}_0)$. Finally, since 
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{\Pi} \bm{x} = (\bm{\Pi}_\alpha \bm{x})_{\alpha \in A},
    \end{align*}
    the result follows.

results.statement:
\label{prop:separation-continuous}
    Let $(X_\alpha, d_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ be a countable family of metric spaces and let $X = \bigsqcup_{\alpha\in A} X_\alpha$ be endowed with the disjoint union metric, $d:X\times X \to [0,\infty]$. Moreover, let $(\mathscr{T}(X_\alpha), \mathscr{d}_{H,\alpha})$ be the tuple Hausdorff space induced by $(X_\alpha, d_\alpha)$ and $(\mathscr{T}(X),\mathscr{d}_H)$ be the tuple Hausdorff space induced by $d$. Then
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item For each $\alpha \in A$, the $\alpha$-separation operator $\bm{\Pi}_\alpha : \mathscr{T}(X) \to \mathscr{T}(X_\alpha)$ is continuous. \label{prop:separation-1}
        \item The separation operator $\bm{\Pi}:\mathscr{T}(X) \to \prod_{\alpha \in A} \mathscr{T}(X_\alpha)$ is continuous whenever $\prod_{\alpha \in A} \mathscr{T}(X_\alpha)$ is endowed with the sup product metric
        \begin{align*}
            D((\bm{y}_\alpha)_\alpha,(\bm{z}_\alpha)_\alpha) = \sup_{\alpha\in A}\mathscr{d}_{H,\alpha}(\bm{y}_\alpha,\bm{z}_\alpha).
        \end{align*}
        \label{prop:separation-2}
    \end{enumerate}

\label{defn:minimal-sort}\index{minimal permutation}
    For a tuple $\bm{x} \in \mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R})$ and one of the usual orders on $\mathbb{R}$, $\preceq \in \{\leq, \geq\}$, define the recursion
    \begin{align}
        D_0 &= \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})\\
        \pi_k &= \min\{i \in D_{k-1} \mid \bm{x}(i) = \min_{\preceq}\{\bm{x}(j) \mid j \in D_{k-1}\}\}\label{eq:permute-pointwise}\\
        D_k &= D_{k-1} \setminus \{\pi_k\}
    \end{align}
for $k \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})$, where $\min_{\preceq}$ denotes the minimum with respect to $\preceq$, while the outer minimum in \eqref{eq:permute-pointwise} is taken with respect to the usual order on $\mathbb{N}$. We define the permutation map $\pi^\preceq:\mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R}) \to \mathscr{P}$ by 
\begin{align*}
    \pi^\preceq(\bm{x})&:\mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}) \to \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})\\ 
    \pi^\preceq(\bm{x})(i) &= \pi_i,
\end{align*}
with $(\pi_i)_{i\in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})}$ defined for each $\bm{x}$ recursively by the schema in \eqref{eq:permute-pointwise}. We call the mapping $\pi^\preceq$ the $\preceq$-minimal permutation map.

steps.text:
We must show that for any $B\in \mathcal{B}(\mathscr{P})$, it holds that 
    \begin{align*}
        (\pi^\preceq)^{-1}(B) \in \mathcal{B}(\mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R})). 
    \end{align*}
    Since $\mathscr{P}$ is countable (Proposition~\ref{prop:permutations-countable}), we first notice that $\mathcal{B}(\mathscr{P})$ is equal to the power set, $\wp(\mathscr{P})$, as each singleton $\{\pi\}\in \mathcal{B}(\mathscr{P})$ and each subset $B \subseteq \mathscr{P}$ is countable and may therefore be written as
    \begin{align*}
        B = \bigcup_{\pi \in B} \{\pi\}. 
    \end{align*}
    As $\sigma$-algebras are closed under countable unions, it suffices to prove that 
    \begin{align*}
        (\pi^\preceq)^{-1}(\{\pi\}) \in \mathcal{B}(\mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R})),
    \end{align*}
    for any $\pi \in \mathscr{P}$. Let $\mathscr{W}_\pi$ be the set defined by 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{W}_\pi = (\pi^\preceq)^{-1}(\{\pi\}) =\{\bm{x} \in \mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R}) \mid \pi^\preceq(\bm{x}) = \pi\}. 
    \end{align*}
    Since $\ell(\pi)$ is fixed, one has
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{W}_\pi = \{\bm{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{\ell(\pi)} \mid \pi^\preceq(\bm{x}) = \pi\} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{\ell(\pi)}. 
    \end{align*}
    Any set that is Borel in $\mathbb{R}^n$ for some $n \in \mathbb{N}$ will also be Borel in $\mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R})$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:tuple-space-measurable-2}. To this end, let $n = \ell(\pi)$ and define
    \begin{align*}
        D_{k-1}^\pi =\{\pi(j) \mid j \in \{k, \dots, n\}\},
    \end{align*}
    and, by extension, the sets 
    \begin{align*}
        A^\pi_k =\bigcap_{j \in D_{k-1}^\pi} \{\bm{x}\in \mathbb{R}^n \mid \bm{x}(\pi(k))\preceq\bm{x}(j)\}.
    \end{align*}
    Each $A_k^\pi$ is measurable because, for every fixed $j$, the set $\{\bm{x}\in\mathbb{R}^n\mid\bm{x}(\pi(k))\preceq\bm{x}(j)\}$ is Borel. Moreover, define
    \begin{align*}
        B^{\pi}_k = \bigcap_{\substack{j \in D^\pi_{k-1}\\j < \pi(k)}} \{\bm{x}\in \mathbb{R}^n \mid \bm{x}(\pi(k)) \neq \bm{x}(j)\},
    \end{align*}
    which is measurable as the diagonal is closed in $\mathbb{R}^2$, and finite intersections of measurable sets are measurable. In addition, 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{W}_\pi =\bigcap_{k = 1}^n (A^\pi_k \cap B^\pi_k).
    \end{align*}
    To see why, note that $A^\pi_k$ contains the tuples for which $\pi(k)$ attains the $k^{\mathrm{th}}$ selected minimum. The set $B^\pi_k$, in turn, enforces the tie-breaking rule by preferring the smallest original index. To see this, if $j \in D_{k-1}^\pi = \{\pi(k), \dots, \pi(n)\}$, then we may write $j = \pi(m)$ for some $m \geq k$; the condition $j < \pi(k)$ forces $m > k$. Consequently, we may write 
    \begin{align*}
        B_k^\pi = \bigcap_{\substack{m \in \{k+1, \dots, n\}\\ \pi(m) < \pi(k)}}\{\bm{x}\in \mathbb{R}^n \mid \bm{x}(\pi(k))\neq \bm{x}(\pi(m))  \},
    \end{align*}
    where the set
    \begin{align*}
        \{\bm{x}\in \mathbb{R}^n \mid \bm{x}(\pi(k))\neq \bm{x}(\pi(m))\}
    \end{align*}
    consists of the tuples in which these two entries differ. If there were an $m \in \{k+1, \dots, n\}$ such that $\bm{x}(\pi(k)) = \bm{x}(\pi(m))$ but $\pi(m) < \pi(k)$, then $\pi^\preceq(\bm{x})$ would have selected $\pi(m)$ before $\pi(k)$, a contradiction. The sets $A_k^\pi$ and $B_k^\pi$ are Borel for each $k \in \{1,\dots, n\}$. Thus $\mathscr{W}_\pi$ is a finite intersection of Borel sets and hence Borel.

