results.statement:
\label{lem:cumulative-summation-continuous}
    Let $(\mathscr{T}([0,\infty)), \mathscr{d}_H)$ denote the tuple Hausdorff space over the non-negative reals and define the cumulative summation\index{cumulative summation} function by 
    \begin{align*}
        &\bm{\Sigma} : \mathscr{T}([0,\infty)) \to \mathscr{T}([0,\infty))\\
        &\bm{\Sigma}(\bm{x})(i) = \sum_{j=1}^i \bm{x}(j),
    \end{align*}
    where $i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})$. Then $\bm{\Sigma}$ is continuous.

steps.text:
We first show that $\mathscr{Q}((\bm{Q}^s, \bm{p}^s),\allowbreak (\bm{Q}^b, \bm{p}^b)) \leq\allowbreak \max_{p \in \mathbb{R}} \min(S(p), D(p))$. If $\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}^s, \bm{p}^b) = \emptyset$, then $\mathscr{Q}((\bm{Q}^s, \bm{p}^s),\allowbreak (\bm{Q}^b, \bm{p}^b)) = 0 \leq\allowbreak \max_{p \in \mathbb{R}} \min(S(p), D(p))$ because $S(p) \geq 0$ and $D(p) \geq 0$ for all $p \in \mathbb{R}$. Suppose therefore that $\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}^s, \bm{p}^b) \neq \emptyset$, and let $(i, j) \in \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}^s, \bm{p}^b)$ be arbitrary. By \eqref{eq:index-mapping}, we have $\bm{p}^s(i) \leq \bm{p}^b(j)$, so we may choose a price $p \in [\bm{p}^s(i), \bm{p}^b(j)]$. Since $\bm{p}^s \in \mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}, \leq)$ is sorted in ascending order and $\bm{p}^b \in \mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}, \geq)$ is sorted in descending order, every $r \in \{1, \dots, i\}$ satisfies $\bm{p}^s(r) \leq \bm{p}^s(i) \leq p$, and every $r \in \{1, \dots, j\}$ satisfies $\bm{p}^b(r) \geq \bm{p}^b(j) \geq p$. Because all order quantities are non-negative, it follows that
    \begin{align*}
        S(p) \geq \sum_{r=1}^{i} \bm{q}^s(r) = \bm{Q}^s(i)
        \qquad \text{and} \qquad
        D(p) \geq \sum_{r=1}^{j} \bm{q}^b(r) = \bm{Q}^b(j),
    \end{align*}
    and hence
    \begin{align*}
        \min(\bm{Q}^s(i), \bm{Q}^b(j)) \leq \min(S(p), D(p)) \leq \max_{p^\prime \in \mathbb{R}} \min(S(p^\prime), D(p^\prime)).
    \end{align*}
    Taking the maximum over $(i, j) \in \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}^s, \bm{p}^b)$ yields $\mathscr{Q}((\bm{Q}^s, \bm{p}^s),\allowbreak (\bm{Q}^b, \bm{p}^b)) \leq\allowbreak \max_{p \in \mathbb{R}} \min(S(p), D(p))$.

For the reverse inequality, fix $p \in \mathbb{R}$. If $\min(S(p), D(p)) = 0$, then $\min(S(p), D(p)) \leq\allowbreak \mathscr{Q}((\bm{Q}^s, \bm{p}^s),\allowbreak (\bm{Q}^b, \bm{p}^b))$ holds immediately. Suppose therefore that $\min(S(p), D(p)) > 0$. Then both $\{r \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{p}^s) \mid \bm{p}^s(r) \leq p\}$ and $\{r \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{p}^b) \mid\allowbreak \bm{p}^b(r) \geq p\}$ are non-empty finite sets; let $i$ and $j$ denote their respective maximum elements. Because $\bm{p}^s$ is non-decreasing and $\bm{p}^b$ is non-increasing, the index sets of qualifying orders are $\{1, \dots, i\}$ and $\{1, \dots, j\}$, which implies that
    \begin{align*}
        S(p) = \sum_{r=1}^{i} \bm{q}^s(r) = \bm{Q}^s(i)
        \qquad \text{and} \qquad
        D(p) = \sum_{r=1}^{j} \bm{q}^b(r) = \bm{Q}^b(j).
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, by construction we have $\bm{p}^s(i) \leq p \leq \bm{p}^b(j)$, so $(i, j) \in \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}^s, \bm{p}^b)$, and consequently
    \begin{align*}
        \min\bigl(S(p), D(p)\bigr) = \min\bigl(\bm{Q}^s(i), \bm{Q}^b(j)\bigr) \leq \mathscr{Q}\bigl((\bm{Q}^s, \bm{p}^s), (\bm{Q}^b, \bm{p}^b)\bigr).
    \end{align*}
    Taking the maximum over $p \in \mathbb{R}$ establishes the reverse inequality, as desired.

proof.text:
We must show that the maximum $\max_{p \in \mathbb{R}} \min(S(p), D(p))$ exists and is equal to $\mathscr{Q}((\bm{Q}^s, \bm{p}^s),\allowbreak (\bm{Q}^b, \bm{p}^b))$. First, note that because $\mathrm{domain}(\bm{p}^s)$ and $\mathrm{domain}(\bm{p}^b)$ are finite sets, the functions $p \mapsto S(p)$ and $p \mapsto D(p)$, and therefore $p \mapsto \min(S(p), D(p))$, take only finitely many values as $p$ ranges over $\mathbb{R}$. Consequently, $\max_{p \in \mathbb{R}} \min(S(p), D(p))$ exists.

    We first show that $\mathscr{Q}((\bm{Q}^s, \bm{p}^s),\allowbreak (\bm{Q}^b, \bm{p}^b)) \leq\allowbreak \max_{p \in \mathbb{R}} \min(S(p), D(p))$. If $\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}^s, \bm{p}^b) = \emptyset$, then $\mathscr{Q}((\bm{Q}^s, \bm{p}^s),\allowbreak (\bm{Q}^b, \bm{p}^b)) = 0 \leq\allowbreak \max_{p \in \mathbb{R}} \min(S(p), D(p))$ because $S(p) \geq 0$ and $D(p) \geq 0$ for all $p \in \mathbb{R}$. Suppose therefore that $\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}^s, \bm{p}^b) \neq \emptyset$, and let $(i, j) \in \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}^s, \bm{p}^b)$ be arbitrary. By \eqref{eq:index-mapping}, we have $\bm{p}^s(i) \leq \bm{p}^b(j)$, so we may choose a price $p \in [\bm{p}^s(i), \bm{p}^b(j)]$. Since $\bm{p}^s \in \mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}, \leq)$ is sorted in ascending order and $\bm{p}^b \in \mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}, \geq)$ is sorted in descending order, every $r \in \{1, \dots, i\}$ satisfies $\bm{p}^s(r) \leq \bm{p}^s(i) \leq p$, and every $r \in \{1, \dots, j\}$ satisfies $\bm{p}^b(r) \geq \bm{p}^b(j) \geq p$. Because all order quantities are non-negative, it follows that
    \begin{align*}
        S(p) \geq \sum_{r=1}^{i} \bm{q}^s(r) = \bm{Q}^s(i)
        \qquad \text{and} \qquad
        D(p) \geq \sum_{r=1}^{j} \bm{q}^b(r) = \bm{Q}^b(j),
    \end{align*}
    and hence
    \begin{align*}
        \min(\bm{Q}^s(i), \bm{Q}^b(j)) \leq \min(S(p), D(p)) \leq \max_{p^\prime \in \mathbb{R}} \min(S(p^\prime), D(p^\prime)).
    \end{align*}
    Taking the maximum over $(i, j) \in \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}^s, \bm{p}^b)$ yields $\mathscr{Q}((\bm{Q}^s, \bm{p}^s),\allowbreak (\bm{Q}^b, \bm{p}^b)) \leq\allowbreak \max_{p \in \mathbb{R}} \min(S(p), D(p))$.

    For the reverse inequality, fix $p \in \mathbb{R}$. If $\min(S(p), D(p)) = 0$, then $\min(S(p), D(p)) \leq\allowbreak \mathscr{Q}((\bm{Q}^s, \bm{p}^s),\allowbreak (\bm{Q}^b, \bm{p}^b))$ holds immediately. Suppose therefore that $\min(S(p), D(p)) > 0$. Then both $\{r \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{p}^s) \mid \bm{p}^s(r) \leq p\}$ and $\{r \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{p}^b) \mid\allowbreak \bm{p}^b(r) \geq p\}$ are non-empty finite sets; let $i$ and $j$ denote their respective maximum elements. Because $\bm{p}^s$ is non-decreasing and $\bm{p}^b$ is non-increasing, the index sets of qualifying orders are $\{1, \dots, i\}$ and $\{1, \dots, j\}$, which implies that
    \begin{align*}
        S(p) = \sum_{r=1}^{i} \bm{q}^s(r) = \bm{Q}^s(i)
        \qquad \text{and} \qquad
        D(p) = \sum_{r=1}^{j} \bm{q}^b(r) = \bm{Q}^b(j).
    \end{align*}
    Moreover, by construction we have $\bm{p}^s(i) \leq p \leq \bm{p}^b(j)$, so $(i, j) \in \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}^s, \bm{p}^b)$, and consequently
    \begin{align*}
        \min\bigl(S(p), D(p)\bigr) = \min\bigl(\bm{Q}^s(i), \bm{Q}^b(j)\bigr) \leq \mathscr{Q}\bigl((\bm{Q}^s, \bm{p}^s), (\bm{Q}^b, \bm{p}^b)\bigr).
    \end{align*}
    Taking the maximum over $p \in \mathbb{R}$ establishes the reverse inequality, as desired.

results.statement:
\label{prop:executable-volume-uniform-price}
    Let $\bm{p}^s\in\mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R},\leq)$ and $\bm{p}^b\in\mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R},\geq)$, let $\bm{q}^s,\bm{q}^b\in\mathscr{T}([0,\infty))$ with $\ell(\bm{q}^s)=\ell(\bm{p}^s)$ and $\ell(\bm{q}^b)=\ell(\bm{p}^b)$, let $\bm{Q}^s=\bm{\Sigma}(\bm{q}^s)$ and $\bm{Q}^b=\bm{\Sigma}(\bm{q}^b)$, and define, for $p\in\mathbb{R}$,
    \begin{align*}
        S(p)=\sum_{i\in\mathrm{domain}(\bm{p}^s):\,\bm{p}^s(i)\leq p}\bm{q}^s(i),
        \qquad
        D(p)=\sum_{j\in\mathrm{domain}(\bm{p}^b):\,\bm{p}^b(j)\geq p}\bm{q}^b(j).
    \end{align*}
    Then
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{Q}((\bm{Q}^s,\bm{p}^s), (\bm{Q}^b,\bm{p}^b))=\max_{p\in\mathbb{R}}\min\bigl(S(p),D(p)\bigr).
    \end{align*}

steps.text:
We must show that for every $B \in \mathcal{B}(\mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N}^2))$, it holds that 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathfrak{I}^{-1}(B) \in \mathcal{B}(\mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}, \leq) \times \mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}, \geq)).
    \end{align*}
    First, note that since $\mathbb{N}^2$ is countable, it follows, by Proposition~\ref{prop:countable-finite-sets}, that $\mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N}^2)$ is countable. Moreover, since $B \subseteq \mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N}^2)$, $B$ must also be countable. Now, as 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathfrak{I}^{-1}(B) =\bigcup_{\eta \in B}\mathfrak{I}^{-1}(\{\eta\})
    \end{align*}
    is a countable union, it suffices to prove that 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathfrak{I}^{-1}(\{\eta\}) \in \mathcal{B}(\mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}, \leq) \times \mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}, \geq))
    \end{align*}
    for each $\eta \in \mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N}^2)$.

Extending the sets of Lemma~\ref{lem:increasing-decreasing-measurable} by the empty tuple, we set
    \begin{align*}
        I_0 &=\{\bm{e}\},\\
        I_n &= \{\bm{x} \in \mathbb{R}^n \mid \bm{x}(1) \leq \dots \leq \bm{x}(n)\}\quad  \forall n \in \mathbb{N},
    \end{align*}
    and similarly, 
    \begin{align*}
        D_0 &=\{\bm{e}\},\\
        D_m &= \{\bm{x} \in \mathbb{R}^m \mid \bm{x}(1) \geq \dots \geq \bm{x}(m)\}\quad  \forall m \in \mathbb{N}.
    \end{align*}
    The singleton sets $I_0=D_0=\{\bm{e}\}$ are Borel measurable in the zero-dimensional components. By Lemma~\ref{lem:increasing-decreasing-measurable}, $I_n$ and $D_m$ are measurable in $\mathbb{R}^n$ and $\mathbb{R}^m$, endowed with their usual Euclidean Borel $\sigma$-algebras, for all $n,m \in \mathbb{N}$.

Lemma~\ref{lem:tuple-space-measurable-2} also makes every set $I_n$ and every set $D_m$, for $n,m\in\mathbb{N}_0$, measurable in the tuple space. Consequently,
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}, \leq) \times \mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}, \geq) &= \left(\bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}_0} I_n\right) \times \left(\bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}_0} D_n\right)\\
        &=\bigcup_{(n,m)\in \mathbb{N}^2_0} (I_n \times D_m),
    \end{align*}
    with $I_n \times D_m$ being measurable in the product space. Therefore, by Theorem~\ref{thm:measurable-partition}, it suffices to prove that for arbitrary $n,m \in \mathbb{N}_0$, the functions 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathfrak{I}\restriction_{I_n \times D_m}: I_n\times D_m \to \mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N}^2)
    \end{align*}
    are $\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^m)/\wp(\mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N}^2))$-measurable. Fix $n,m\in\mathbb{N}_0$ and $\eta\in\mathscr{F}(\mathbb{N}^2)$. If
    \begin{align*}
        \eta \nsubseteq \{1,\dots,n\}\times\{1,\dots,m\},
    \end{align*}
    then $\mathfrak{I}\restriction_{I_n\times D_m}^{-1}(\{\eta\})=\emptyset$, which is measurable. Therefore, assume that $\eta \subseteq \{1,\dots,n\}\times\{1,\dots,m\}$. For $(i,j) \in \{1,\dots, n\}\times \{1,\dots, m\}$, recall from Lemma~\ref{lem:bounded-sets-measurable} the sets
    \begin{align*}
        L^{i,j}_{n,m} = \{(\bm{x},\bm{y}) \in I_n\times D_m \mid \bm{x}(i) \leq \bm{y}(j)\},
    \end{align*}
    and 
    \begin{align*}
       G^{i,j}_{n,m} = \{(\bm{x},\bm{y}) \in I_n\times D_m \mid \bm{x}(i) > \bm{y}(j)\},
    \end{align*}
    and observe that both are measurable sets by Lemma~\ref{lem:bounded-sets-measurable}. One then verifies, including the case $\eta=\emptyset$, that
    \begin{align}
        \mathfrak{I}\restriction_{I_n \times D_m}^{-1}(\{\eta\}) =  \bigcap_{(i,j) \in \eta} L^{i,j}_{n,m} \cap \bigcap_{\substack{i \in \{1,\dots, n\} \\ j\in \{1,\dots,m\}\\ (i,j) \notin \eta}} G^{i,j}_{n,m},
    \end{align}
    which is a finite intersection of measurable sets. Consequently, the result follows from Lemma~\ref{lem:tuple-space-measurable-2}, applied with $k=2$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:measurable-partition}.

