results.statement:
\label{thm:equilibrium-quantity-measurable}
    Let $\{\leq, \geq\}$ denote the usual order relations on $\mathbb{R}$, and 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathfrak{C}(R) = \{(\bm{x},\bm{y}) \in \mathscr{S}([0,\infty), \leq) \times \mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}, R) \mid \ell(\bm{x}) = \ell(\bm{y})\} 
    \end{align*}
    for $R \in \{\leq, \geq\}$. Moreover, endow $\mathfrak{C}(R)$ with the product of the tuple Hausdorff metrics, and let $\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime) = \{(i,j) \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{p}) \times \mathrm{domain}(\bm{p}^\prime)\mid \bm{p}(i) \leq \bm{p}^\prime(j)\}$. Then the function $\mathscr{Q}:\mathfrak{C}(\leq)\times \mathfrak{C}(\geq) \to [0,\infty)$ defined by 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{Q}((\bm{Q},\bm{p}), (\bm{Q}^\prime, \bm{p}^\prime)) =\begin{cases}
            0 & \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime) =\emptyset\\ 
            \max \{\min(\bm{Q}(i),\bm{Q}^\prime(j)) \mid (i,j) \in \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)\} & \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime) \neq \emptyset,
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    is measurable.

steps.text:
To prove (i), we must show that for every $z=((\bm{Q},\bm{p}),(\bm{Q}^\prime,\bm{p}^\prime)) \in \mathfrak{C}(\leq) \times \mathfrak{C}(\geq)$ and every sequence $(z_k)_{k\in\mathbb{N}}$ in $\mathfrak{C}(\leq) \times \mathfrak{C}(\geq)$ converging to $z$, it holds that $\limsup_{k\to\infty} \mathscr{Q}(z_k) \leq \mathscr{Q}(z)$. Let $z_k=((\bm{Q}_k,\bm{p}_k),(\bm{Q}^\prime_k,\bm{p}^\prime_k))$, $k\in\mathbb{N}$, converge to $z$. By the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery}, the fixed-length layers are clopen and carry the usual Euclidean topology, so there exists $K\in\mathbb{N}$ such that, for all $k\geq K$, the tuples $\bm{p}_k$ and $\bm{p}^\prime_k$ have the constant lengths $n=\ell(\bm{p})$ and $m=\ell(\bm{p}^\prime)$, and the coordinates of $\bm{Q}_k$, $\bm{p}_k$, $\bm{Q}^\prime_k$, and $\bm{p}^\prime_k$ converge pointwise to those of $\bm{Q}$, $\bm{p}$, $\bm{Q}^\prime$, and $\bm{p}^\prime$. If a pair $(i,j)\in\{1,\dots,n\}\times\{1,\dots,m\}$ lies outside $\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)$, then $\bm{p}(i)>\bm{p}^\prime(j)$, and hence $\bm{p}_k(i)>\bm{p}^\prime_k(j)$ for all sufficiently large $k$. Because $\{1,\dots,n\}\times\{1,\dots,m\}$ is finite, we may enlarge $K$ so that
    \begin{align*}
        \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}_k,\bm{p}^\prime_k)\subseteq\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)
        \qquad\text{for all } k\geq K.
    \end{align*}
    For each $k\geq K$, define
    \begin{align*}
        g_k = \begin{cases}
            0, & \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime) = \emptyset,\\
            \max\bigl\{\min(\bm{Q}_k(i),\bm{Q}^\prime_k(j))\bigm| (i,j)\in\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)\bigr\}, & \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime) \neq \emptyset.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    Since all cumulative quantities are non-negative, the inclusion $\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}_k,\bm{p}^\prime_k)\subseteq\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)$ implies that $\mathscr{Q}(z_k)\leq g_k$ for all $k \geq K$. Moreover, because the maximum and minimum over a fixed finite index set are continuous, we have $\lim_{k\to\infty} g_k = \mathscr{Q}(z)$. Consequently,
    \begin{align*}
        \limsup_{k\to\infty}\mathscr{Q}(z_k) \leq \lim_{k\to\infty} g_k = \mathscr{Q}(z),
    \end{align*}
    which proves (i).

To prove (ii), suppose in addition that $\bm{p}(i)\neq\bm{p}^\prime(j)$ for all $(i,j)\in\mathrm{domain}(\bm{p})\times\mathrm{domain}(\bm{p}^\prime)$. Then every $(i,j)\in\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)$ satisfies the strict inequality $\bm{p}(i)<\bm{p}^\prime(j)$, and hence $\bm{p}_k(i)<\bm{p}^\prime_k(j)$ for all sufficiently large $k$. Enlarging $K$ if necessary, we obtain $\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}_k,\bm{p}^\prime_k) = \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)$ for all $k \geq K$, which yields
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{k\to\infty} \mathscr{Q}(z_k) = \lim_{k\to\infty} g_k = \mathscr{Q}(z),
    \end{align*}
    establishing continuity at $z$.

proof.text:
To prove (i), we must show that for every $z=((\bm{Q},\bm{p}),(\bm{Q}^\prime,\bm{p}^\prime)) \in \mathfrak{C}(\leq) \times \mathfrak{C}(\geq)$ and every sequence $(z_k)_{k\in\mathbb{N}}$ in $\mathfrak{C}(\leq) \times \mathfrak{C}(\geq)$ converging to $z$, it holds that $\limsup_{k\to\infty} \mathscr{Q}(z_k) \leq \mathscr{Q}(z)$. Let $z_k=((\bm{Q}_k,\bm{p}_k),(\bm{Q}^\prime_k,\bm{p}^\prime_k))$, $k\in\mathbb{N}$, converge to $z$. By the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery}, the fixed-length layers are clopen and carry the usual Euclidean topology, so there exists $K\in\mathbb{N}$ such that, for all $k\geq K$, the tuples $\bm{p}_k$ and $\bm{p}^\prime_k$ have the constant lengths $n=\ell(\bm{p})$ and $m=\ell(\bm{p}^\prime)$, and the coordinates of $\bm{Q}_k$, $\bm{p}_k$, $\bm{Q}^\prime_k$, and $\bm{p}^\prime_k$ converge pointwise to those of $\bm{Q}$, $\bm{p}$, $\bm{Q}^\prime$, and $\bm{p}^\prime$. If a pair $(i,j)\in\{1,\dots,n\}\times\{1,\dots,m\}$ lies outside $\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)$, then $\bm{p}(i)>\bm{p}^\prime(j)$, and hence $\bm{p}_k(i)>\bm{p}^\prime_k(j)$ for all sufficiently large $k$. Because $\{1,\dots,n\}\times\{1,\dots,m\}$ is finite, we may enlarge $K$ so that
    \begin{align*}
        \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}_k,\bm{p}^\prime_k)\subseteq\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)
        \qquad\text{for all } k\geq K.
    \end{align*}
    For each $k\geq K$, define
    \begin{align*}
        g_k = \begin{cases}
            0, & \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime) = \emptyset,\\
            \max\bigl\{\min(\bm{Q}_k(i),\bm{Q}^\prime_k(j))\bigm| (i,j)\in\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)\bigr\}, & \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime) \neq \emptyset.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    Since all cumulative quantities are non-negative, the inclusion $\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}_k,\bm{p}^\prime_k)\subseteq\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)$ implies that $\mathscr{Q}(z_k)\leq g_k$ for all $k \geq K$. Moreover, because the maximum and minimum over a fixed finite index set are continuous, we have $\lim_{k\to\infty} g_k = \mathscr{Q}(z)$. Consequently,
    \begin{align*}
        \limsup_{k\to\infty}\mathscr{Q}(z_k) \leq \lim_{k\to\infty} g_k = \mathscr{Q}(z),
    \end{align*}
    which proves (i).

    To prove (ii), suppose in addition that $\bm{p}(i)\neq\bm{p}^\prime(j)$ for all $(i,j)\in\mathrm{domain}(\bm{p})\times\mathrm{domain}(\bm{p}^\prime)$. Then every $(i,j)\in\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)$ satisfies the strict inequality $\bm{p}(i)<\bm{p}^\prime(j)$, and hence $\bm{p}_k(i)<\bm{p}^\prime_k(j)$ for all sufficiently large $k$. Enlarging $K$ if necessary, we obtain $\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}_k,\bm{p}^\prime_k) = \mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)$ for all $k \geq K$, which yields
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{k\to\infty} \mathscr{Q}(z_k) = \lim_{k\to\infty} g_k = \mathscr{Q}(z),
    \end{align*}
    establishing continuity at $z$.

    Finally, to prove (iii), let $z=((\bm{Q},\bm{p}),(\bm{Q}^\prime,\bm{p}^\prime))$ be the point specified in (iii), and for each $k \in \mathbb{N}$ define $z_k=((\bm{Q},\bm{p}_k),(\bm{Q}^\prime,\bm{p}^\prime))$, where $\ell(\bm{p}_k)=1$ and $\bm{p}_k(1)=1/k$. Then $z_k \to z$ as $k \to \infty$, but $\bm{p}_k(1) = 1/k > 0 = \bm{p}^\prime(1)$ for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$. Thus $\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p}_k,\bm{p}^\prime)=\emptyset$ and $\mathscr{Q}(z_k)=0$ for all $k \in \mathbb{N}$, whereas $\mathfrak{I}(\bm{p},\bm{p}^\prime)=\{(1,1)\}$ and $\mathscr{Q}(z)=\min(1,1)=1$, proving that $\mathscr{Q}$ is discontinuous at $z$, as desired.

steps.text:
Third, by Lemma~\ref{lem:cumulative-summation-continuous}, the cumulative summation map $\bm{\Sigma} : \mathscr{T}([0,\infty)) \to \mathscr{T}([0,\infty))$ is continuous. Since the sorted price tuples $\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}$ and $\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}$ belong to $\mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}, \leq)$ and $\mathscr{S}(\mathbb{R}, \geq)$, the cumulative quantities $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}}$ and $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}}$ are non-decreasing, and reindexing together with $\bm{\Sigma}$ preserves tuple lengths, the image of the map
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{m} \mapsto \Bigl(\bigl(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m}), \widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m})\bigr), \bigl(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m}), \widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m})\bigr)\Bigr)
    \end{align*}
    lies in $\mathfrak{C}(\leq) \times \mathfrak{C}(\geq)$. By the product identity for Borel $\sigma$-algebras on separable metric spaces noted in Subsection~\ref{subsec:step-3-sorting}, componentwise measurability implies that this map is Borel measurable into $\mathfrak{C}(\leq) \times \mathfrak{C}(\geq)$. Composing it with the Borel measurable functional $\mathscr{Q}$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:equilibrium-quantity-measurable} proves that $\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}$ is Borel measurable, as desired.

First, by Corollary~\ref{cor:state-compatible-volume-measurable}, the tuple maps $\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}$, $\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}$, $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}}$, and $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}}$, as well as the scalar functional $\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}$, are Borel measurable. If $\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})>0$ and $(i^*,j^*) \in \mathfrak{I}(\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}, \widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}})$ attains the maximum in the definition of $\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})$, then $\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})=\min(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}}(i^*),\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}}(j^*))$, so $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}}(i^*)\geq\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})$ and $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}}(j^*)\geq\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})$. Hence the minimal indices $\iota^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m})\leq i^*$ and $\iota^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m})\leq j^*$ exist in $\mathrm{domain}(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}})$ and $\mathrm{domain}(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}})$, respectively, and $\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}$ is well-defined. Moreover, since the sell prices are sorted in ascending order and the buy prices in descending order, we have
\begin{align*}
    \widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}(\iota^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m}))\leq\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}(i^*)\leq\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}(j^*)\leq\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}(\iota^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m})),
\end{align*}
so the midpoint lies between two price-compatible marginal prices.

On $A_+$, we decompose the domain by the lengths of the separated sell and buy sub-tuples. For each pair $(n_s, n_b) \in \mathbb{N}^2$, define
\begin{align*}
    A_{n_s, n_b} = \bigl\{\bm{m} \in A_+ \;\big|\; \ell(\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m})) = n_s \;\land\; \ell(\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m})) = n_b\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
Since the fixed-length layers of $\mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R})$ are clopen by the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery} (Theorem~\ref{thm:the-standard-machinery}) and the maps $\bm{m} \mapsto \widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m})$ and $\bm{m} \mapsto \widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m})$ are Borel measurable, the sets $(A_{n_s, n_b})_{(n_s, n_b) \in \mathbb{N}^2}$, together with $A_0$, form a countable measurable partition of $\mathscr{T}(O)$.

Fix $(n_s, n_b) \in \mathbb{N}^2$, and recall from the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:equilibrium-quantity-measurable} the continuous coordinate evaluation maps $\mathrm{ev}_i : \mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R}) \to \mathbb{R}$, with $\mathrm{ev}_0 \equiv 0$. For each $i \in \{1, \dots, n_s\}$ and $j \in \{1, \dots, n_b\}$, define the subset
\begin{align*}
    B_{n_s, n_b}^{i, j} = \bigl\{\bm{m} \in A_{n_s, n_b} \;\big|\; \iota^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m}) = i \;\land\; \iota^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m}) = j\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
By the definition of the minimal index $\iota^a(\bm{m})$ and the monotonicity of $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^a(\bm{m})$, we have
\begin{align*}
    B_{n_s, n_b}^{i, j}
    &= \bigl\{\bm{m} \in A_{n_s, n_b} \;\big|\; \mathrm{ev}_{i-1}(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m})) < \mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m}) \leq \mathrm{ev}_i(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m}))\bigr\}\\
    &\quad \cap \bigl\{\bm{m} \in A_{n_s, n_b} \;\big|\; \mathrm{ev}_{j-1}(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m})) < \mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m}) \leq \mathrm{ev}_j(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m}))\bigr\},
\end{align*}
which is a finite intersection of Borel sets and therefore Borel measurable. Moreover, on each cell $B_{n_s, n_b}^{i, j}$, the map $\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}$ coincides with the fixed linear combination
\begin{align*}
    \bm{m} \mapsto \frac{1}{2}\Bigl(\mathrm{ev}_i\bigl(\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m})\bigr) + \mathrm{ev}_j\bigl(\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m})\bigr)\Bigr),
\end{align*}
which is a composition of Borel measurable maps and hence Borel measurable. Because the family $\{A_0\} \cup \{B_{n_s, n_b}^{i, j} \mid (n_s, n_b) \in \mathbb{N}^2, 1 \leq i \leq n_s, 1 \leq j \leq n_b\}$ is a countable measurable partition of $\mathscr{T}(O)$, Theorem~\ref{thm:measurable-partition} implies that $\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}$ is Borel measurable. Finally, since $\mathbb{R} \times [0, \infty)$ is separable, $\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R} \times [0, \infty)) = \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}) \otimes \mathcal{B}([0, \infty))$, and componentwise measurability yields the joint Borel measurability of $(\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}, \mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}})$, as desired.

proof.text:
We must show that $\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}$ is well-defined on $\mathscr{T}(O)$ and that the paired map $(\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}},\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}) : \mathscr{T}(O) \to \mathbb{R} \times [0,\infty)$ is Borel measurable.

First, by Corollary~\ref{cor:state-compatible-volume-measurable}, the tuple maps $\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}$, $\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}$, $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}}$, and $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}}$, as well as the scalar functional $\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}$, are Borel measurable. If $\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})>0$ and $(i^*,j^*) \in \mathfrak{I}(\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}, \widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}})$ attains the maximum in the definition of $\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})$, then $\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})=\min(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}}(i^*),\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}}(j^*))$, so $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}}(i^*)\geq\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})$ and $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}}(j^*)\geq\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})$. Hence the minimal indices $\iota^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m})\leq i^*$ and $\iota^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m})\leq j^*$ exist in $\mathrm{domain}(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}})$ and $\mathrm{domain}(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}})$, respectively, and $\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}$ is well-defined. Moreover, since the sell prices are sorted in ascending order and the buy prices in descending order, we have
\begin{align*}
    \widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}(\iota^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m}))\leq\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}(i^*)\leq\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}(j^*)\leq\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}(\iota^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m})),
\end{align*}
so the midpoint lies between two price-compatible marginal prices.

It remains to show that $\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}} : \mathscr{T}(O) \to \mathbb{R}$ is Borel measurable. Set $A_0 = \{\bm{m} \in \mathscr{T}(O) \mid \mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m}) = 0\}$ and $A_+ = \{\bm{m} \in \mathscr{T}(O) \mid \mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m}) > 0\}$. Because $\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}$ is Borel measurable by Corollary~\ref{cor:state-compatible-volume-measurable}, both $A_0$ and $A_+$ belong to $\mathcal{B}(\mathscr{T}(O))$. On $A_0$, the map $\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}$ is identically zero and hence measurable.

On $A_+$, we decompose the domain by the lengths of the separated sell and buy sub-tuples. For each pair $(n_s, n_b) \in \mathbb{N}^2$, define
\begin{align*}
    A_{n_s, n_b} = \bigl\{\bm{m} \in A_+ \;\big|\; \ell(\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m})) = n_s \;\land\; \ell(\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m})) = n_b\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
Since the fixed-length layers of $\mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R})$ are clopen by the \hyperref[thm:the-standard-machinery]{standard machinery} (Theorem~\ref{thm:the-standard-machinery}) and the maps $\bm{m} \mapsto \widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m})$ and $\bm{m} \mapsto \widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m})$ are Borel measurable, the sets $(A_{n_s, n_b})_{(n_s, n_b) \in \mathbb{N}^2}$, together with $A_0$, form a countable measurable partition of $\mathscr{T}(O)$.

Fix $(n_s, n_b) \in \mathbb{N}^2$, and recall from the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:equilibrium-quantity-measurable} the continuous coordinate evaluation maps $\mathrm{ev}_i : \mathscr{T}(\mathbb{R}) \to \mathbb{R}$, with $\mathrm{ev}_0 \equiv 0$. For each $i \in \{1, \dots, n_s\}$ and $j \in \{1, \dots, n_b\}$, define the subset
\begin{align*}
    B_{n_s, n_b}^{i, j} = \bigl\{\bm{m} \in A_{n_s, n_b} \;\big|\; \iota^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m}) = i \;\land\; \iota^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m}) = j\bigr\}.
\end{align*}
By the definition of the minimal index $\iota^a(\bm{m})$ and the monotonicity of $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^a(\bm{m})$, we have
\begin{align*}
    B_{n_s, n_b}^{i, j}
    &= \bigl\{\bm{m} \in A_{n_s, n_b} \;\big|\; \mathrm{ev}_{i-1}(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m})) < \mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m}) \leq \mathrm{ev}_i(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m}))\bigr\}\\
    &\quad \cap \bigl\{\bm{m} \in A_{n_s, n_b} \;\big|\; \mathrm{ev}_{j-1}(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m})) < \mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m}) \leq \mathrm{ev}_j(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m}))\bigr\},
\end{align*}
which is a finite intersection of Borel sets and therefore Borel measurable. Moreover, on each cell $B_{n_s, n_b}^{i, j}$, the map $\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}$ coincides with the fixed linear combination
\begin{align*}
    \bm{m} \mapsto \frac{1}{2}\Bigl(\mathrm{ev}_i\bigl(\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m})\bigr) + \mathrm{ev}_j\bigl(\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m})\bigr)\Bigr),
\end{align*}
which is a composition of Borel measurable maps and hence Borel measurable. Because the family $\{A_0\} \cup \{B_{n_s, n_b}^{i, j} \mid (n_s, n_b) \in \mathbb{N}^2, 1 \leq i \leq n_s, 1 \leq j \leq n_b\}$ is a countable measurable partition of $\mathscr{T}(O)$, Theorem~\ref{thm:measurable-partition} implies that $\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}$ is Borel measurable. Finally, since $\mathbb{R} \times [0, \infty)$ is separable, $\mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R} \times [0, \infty)) = \mathcal{B}(\mathbb{R}) \otimes \mathcal{B}([0, \infty))$, and componentwise measurability yields the joint Borel measurability of $(\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}, \mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}})$, as desired.

results.statement:
\label{cor:state-midpoint-price-measurable}
Let $O=\bigsqcup_{\kappa\in\{\mathrm{buy},\mathrm{sell}\}}\mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty)$ carry the disjoint-union metric induced by the maximum product metric on $\mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty)$, and equip $\mathscr{T}(O)$ with its tuple Hausdorff Borel $\sigma$-algebra. For $\bm{m}\in\mathscr{T}(O)$, let $\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}$, $\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}$, $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{s}}$, $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^{\bm{b}}$, and $\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}$ be the sorted price, cumulative-quantity, and maximum executable volume maps from Corollary~\ref{cor:state-compatible-volume-measurable}. For $a\in\{\bm{s},\bm{b}\}$ and $\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})>0$, let $\iota^a(\bm{m})$ be the least $i\in\mathrm{domain}(\widehat{\bm{Q}}^a)$ such that $\widehat{\bm{Q}}^a(i)\geq\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})$. Then the map $\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}:\mathscr{T}(O)\to\mathbb{R}$ given by
\begin{align*}
\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})=
\begin{cases}
0,
&\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})=0,\\[2pt]
\dfrac{\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{s}}(\iota^{\bm{s}}(\bm{m}))
+\widehat{\bm{p}}^{\bm{b}}(\iota^{\bm{b}}(\bm{m}))}{2},
&\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m})>0,
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
is well-defined, and $(\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}},\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}):\mathscr{T}(O)\to\mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty)$ is Borel measurable for the product Borel $\sigma$-algebra.

\label{lem:state-functionals-permutation-invariant}
    Let $O=\bigsqcup_{\kappa\in\{\mathrm{buy},\mathrm{sell}\}}\mathbb{R}\times[0,\infty)$, and let $\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}:\mathscr{T}(O)\to[0,\infty)$ and $\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}:\mathscr{T}(O)\to\mathbb{R}$ be as in Corollaries~\ref{cor:state-compatible-volume-measurable} and~\ref{cor:state-midpoint-price-measurable}. Then, for every $\bm{m}\in\mathscr{T}(O)$ and every bijection $\rho$ of $\mathrm{domain}(\bm{m})$,
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m}\circ\rho)=\mathscr{Q}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m}),
        \qquad
        \mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m}\circ\rho)=\mathscr{P}_{\mathrm{state}}(\bm{m}).
    \end{align*}

\label{thm:prokhorov-metric-weak-convergence-polish}
    Let $(X,d)$ be a complete and separable metric space, and let $\mathcal{M}(X)$ denote the family of finite measures on $(X,\mathcal{B}(X))$. Let $\rho:\mathcal{M}(X) \times \mathcal{M}(X) \to [0,\infty)$ be the function defined by 
    \begin{align*}
        \rho(\mu, \eta) = \inf \{ \varepsilon > 0 \mid \forall B \in \mathcal{B}(X): \mu(B) \leq \eta(B^\varepsilon) + \varepsilon \mathrm{~and~}\eta(B) \leq \mu(B^\varepsilon) + \varepsilon\}. 
    \end{align*}
    Then $(\mathcal{M}(X), \rho)$ is a metric space, and $\rho$ is called the Prokhorov metric. Moreover, 
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item a sequence $(\mu_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}} \subseteq \mathcal{M}(X)$ converges weakly to $\mu \in \mathcal{M}(X) $ if and only if $\lim_{n \to \infty}\rho(\mu_n,\mu) = 0$; 
        \item $(\mathcal{M}(X), \rho)$ is a complete and separable metric space; and  
        \item $(\mathcal{N}(X), \rho)$ is a complete and separable metric space.
    \end{enumerate}

\label{rem:point-process-measurability-equivalence}
    If $(X,d)$ is complete and separable, the Borel $\sigma$-algebra on $(\mathcal{N}(X),\rho)$ is the evaluation $\sigma$-algebra
    \begin{align*}
        \sigma\{\mu\mapsto\mu(B)\mid B\in\mathcal{B}(X)\}.
    \end{align*}
    Consequently, a map $\xi:\Omega\to\mathcal{N}(X)$ is a Borel random element if and only if $\omega\mapsto\xi(\omega)(B)$ is measurable for every $B\in\mathcal{B}(X)$. We use these equivalent random-element and evaluation-wise formulations of a finite point process interchangeably; see \citet{daley2003introduction} and \citet{kallenberg2017random}.

\label{thm:sufficient-decomposition}
    Let $(X,\Sigma)$ be a standard Borel space. Then every $\xi \in \mathcal{N}(X)$ has the representation 
    \begin{align*}
        \xi = \sum_{k \leq \kappa} \beta_k \delta_{x_k},
    \end{align*}
    where $\kappa \in \mathbb{N}_0$ (the empty sum being $0$ when $\kappa=0$), $\beta_k\in\mathbb{N}$ for every $k\in\{1,\dots,\kappa\}$, the points $x_1,\dots,x_\kappa\in X$ are distinct, and $\delta_x$ is the Dirac measure at $x$. For $\kappa \leq 1$, the representation is unique; otherwise, it is unique up to permutations of $\{1, \dots, \kappa\}$.

steps.text:
\textbf{Proof of \eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-1} and \eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-2}:} It suffices to show \eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-1}, as \eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-2} is analogous. Let $\varepsilon \in (0,1)$ satisfy $\rho(\xi,\eta)<\varepsilon$. By the definition of the Prokhorov metric, there exists $\theta \in (0,\varepsilon)$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \xi(B) \leq \eta(B^\theta) + \theta
    \end{align*}
    for every $B \in \mathcal{B}(X)$. Since $B^\theta \subseteq B^\varepsilon$, it follows that
    \begin{align*}
        \xi(B) \leq \eta(B^\varepsilon) + \theta.
    \end{align*}
    Now $\xi(B)$ and $\eta(B^\varepsilon)$ are integers and $\theta < 1$, so the above inequality implies
    \begin{align*}
        \xi(B) \leq \eta(B^\varepsilon),
    \end{align*}
    which proves \eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-1}.

\textbf{Proof of \eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-3}:} Apply \eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-1} and \eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-2} with $B=X$. Since $X^\varepsilon = X$, we obtain
    \begin{align*}
        \xi(X) \leq \eta(X) \quad \text{and} \quad \eta(X) \leq \xi(X),
    \end{align*}
    which implies that $\xi(X) = \eta(X)$.

proof.text:
\textbf{Proof of \eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-1} and \eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-2}:} It suffices to show \eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-1}, as \eqref{eq:lem-helpful-point-process-2} is analogous. Let $\varepsilon \in (0,1)$ satisfy $\rho(\xi,\eta)<\varepsilon$. By the definition of the Prokhorov metric, there exists $\theta \in (0,\varepsilon)$ such that
