results.statement:
\label{prop:tuple-induced-measure-continuous}
    Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space, $(\mathscr{T}(X), \mathscr{d}_H)$ be the associated tuple Hausdorff space, and $(\mathcal{N}(X), \rho)$ be the space of finite integer-valued measures endowed with the Prokhorov distance. Then the mapping $\mathfrak{m} :\mathscr{T}(X) \to \mathcal{N}(X)$ defined by 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathfrak{m}(\bm{x}) = B \mapsto \sum_{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})} \delta_{\bm{x}(i)}(B),
    \end{align*}
    is continuous.

steps.text:
By Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficient-decomposition} any measure $\xi \in \mathcal{N}(X)$ admits the decomposition 
    \begin{align*}
        \xi =\sum_{k \leq \kappa } \beta_k\delta_{x_k},
    \end{align*}
    with $(\beta_k)_{k \leq \kappa}\subseteq \mathbb{N}$ and $(x_k)_{k\leq \kappa} \subseteq X$. Note that the representation is unique up to a permutation of $\{k \in \mathbb{N} \mid k \leq \kappa\}$; fix an arbitrary enumeration and define the tuple 
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{x}(i) = \begin{cases}
            x_1 & i \in \{1,\dots, \beta_1\} \\
            x_2 & i \in \{ \beta_1 + 1, \dots, \beta_1 +\beta_2\}\\ 
            ~\vdots &~~~\vdots \\
            x_\kappa & i \in \{\sum_{k = 1}^{\kappa-1} \beta_k+1, \dots, \sum_{k \leq \kappa}\beta_k\},
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    which by construction satisfies, for every measurable $B$, 
    \begin{align*}
        \xi(B) =\sum_{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})} \delta_{\bm{x}(i)}(B) =\mathfrak{m}(\bm{x})(B),
    \end{align*}
    as desired.

proof.text:
Let $\xi\in\mathcal{N}(X)$. If $\xi=0$, then the empty tuple $\bm{e}$ satisfies $\mathfrak{m}(\bm{e})=\xi$.
    Hence assume $\xi\neq 0$.

    By Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficient-decomposition} any measure $\xi \in \mathcal{N}(X)$ admits the decomposition 
    \begin{align*}
        \xi =\sum_{k \leq \kappa } \beta_k\delta_{x_k},
    \end{align*}
    with $(\beta_k)_{k \leq \kappa}\subseteq \mathbb{N}$ and $(x_k)_{k\leq \kappa} \subseteq X$. Note that the representation is unique up to a permutation of $\{k \in \mathbb{N} \mid k \leq \kappa\}$; fix an arbitrary enumeration and define the tuple 
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{x}(i) = \begin{cases}
            x_1 & i \in \{1,\dots, \beta_1\} \\
            x_2 & i \in \{ \beta_1 + 1, \dots, \beta_1 +\beta_2\}\\ 
            ~\vdots &~~~\vdots \\
            x_\kappa & i \in \{\sum_{k = 1}^{\kappa-1} \beta_k+1, \dots, \sum_{k \leq \kappa}\beta_k\},
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}
    which by construction satisfies, for every measurable $B$, 
    \begin{align*}
        \xi(B) =\sum_{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x})} \delta_{\bm{x}(i)}(B) =\mathfrak{m}(\bm{x})(B),
    \end{align*}
    as desired.

results.statement:
\label{cor:tuple-induced-measure-permutation-invariant}
    Let $X$ be a metric space, $\mathscr{T}(X)$ be the associated tuple space, and let $\mathcal{N}(X)$ denote the finite integer-valued measures on $X$. Then, for every $\bm{x} \in \mathscr{T}(X)$ it holds that
    \begin{align*}
        \forall \bm{x}^\prime \in \bm{x}^\pi: \mathfrak{m}(\bm{x}^\prime) = \mathfrak{m}(\bm{x}). 
    \end{align*}

steps.text:
Let $\bm{x}^\pi, \bm{y}^\pi \in \mathscr{T}^\pi(X)$ satisfy
    \begin{align*}
        \mathfrak{m}^\pi(\bm{x}^\pi)=\mathfrak{m}^\pi(\bm{y}^\pi).
    \end{align*}
    Choose representatives $\bm{x}\in \bm{x}^\pi$ and $\bm{y}\in \bm{y}^\pi$. Then $\mathfrak{m}(\bm{x})=\mathfrak{m}(\bm{y})$. In particular,
    \begin{align*}
        \ell(\bm{x}) = \mathfrak{m}(\bm{x})(X) = \mathfrak{m}(\bm{y})(X) = \ell(\bm{y}).
    \end{align*}
    For each $a \in X$, define
    \begin{align*}
        I_a = \{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}) \mid \bm{x}(i)=a\}, \qquad J_a = \{j \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{y}) \mid \bm{y}(j)=a\}.
    \end{align*}
    Equality of the induced measures implies $|I_a| = |J_a|$ for every $a \in X$ (evaluate both measures on $\{a\}$, which is measurable in a standard Borel space). Since only finitely many of these sets are non-empty, we may choose bijections
    \begin{align*}
        \sigma_a : I_a \to J_a
    \end{align*}
    for each such $a \in X$. The union of these bijections defines a permutation
    \begin{align*}
        \sigma:\mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}) \to \mathrm{domain}(\bm{y}),
    \end{align*}
    and by construction $\bm{y}(\sigma(i))=\bm{x}(i)$ for every $i$. Hence
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{x} = \bm{y}\circ \sigma,
    \end{align*}
    so $\bm{x} \overset{\pi}{\sim} \bm{y}$ and therefore $\bm{x}^\pi = \bm{y}^\pi$. Thus $\mathfrak{m}^\pi$ is injective.

proof.text:
First, note that $\mathfrak{m}^\pi$ is well-defined, as every $\bm{x}^\prime \in \bm{x}^\pi$ satisfies $\mathfrak{m}(\bm{x}^\prime) = \mathfrak{m}(\bm{x})$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:tuple-measure-surjective}, the mapping is surjective. It therefore remains to prove injectivity.

    Let $\bm{x}^\pi, \bm{y}^\pi \in \mathscr{T}^\pi(X)$ satisfy
    \begin{align*}
        \mathfrak{m}^\pi(\bm{x}^\pi)=\mathfrak{m}^\pi(\bm{y}^\pi).
    \end{align*}
    Choose representatives $\bm{x}\in \bm{x}^\pi$ and $\bm{y}\in \bm{y}^\pi$. Then $\mathfrak{m}(\bm{x})=\mathfrak{m}(\bm{y})$. In particular,
    \begin{align*}
        \ell(\bm{x}) = \mathfrak{m}(\bm{x})(X) = \mathfrak{m}(\bm{y})(X) = \ell(\bm{y}).
    \end{align*}
    For each $a \in X$, define
    \begin{align*}
        I_a = \{i \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}) \mid \bm{x}(i)=a\}, \qquad J_a = \{j \in \mathrm{domain}(\bm{y}) \mid \bm{y}(j)=a\}.
    \end{align*}
    Equality of the induced measures implies $|I_a| = |J_a|$ for every $a \in X$ (evaluate both measures on $\{a\}$, which is measurable in a standard Borel space). Since only finitely many of these sets are non-empty, we may choose bijections
    \begin{align*}
        \sigma_a : I_a \to J_a
    \end{align*}
    for each such $a \in X$. The union of these bijections defines a permutation
    \begin{align*}
        \sigma:\mathrm{domain}(\bm{x}) \to \mathrm{domain}(\bm{y}),
    \end{align*}
    and by construction $\bm{y}(\sigma(i))=\bm{x}(i)$ for every $i$. Hence
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{x} = \bm{y}\circ \sigma,
    \end{align*}
    so $\bm{x} \overset{\pi}{\sim} \bm{y}$ and therefore $\bm{x}^\pi = \bm{y}^\pi$. Thus $\mathfrak{m}^\pi$ is injective.

results.statement:
\label{prop:permuted-tuple-measure-map-bijective}
    Let $X$ be a standard Borel space, let $\mathscr{T}(X)$ be the associated tuple space, and let $\mathcal{N}(X)$ be the space of finite integer-valued measures on $X$. Then, the mapping $\mathfrak{m}^\pi:\mathscr{T}^\pi(X) \to \mathcal{N}(X)$ defined by 
    \begin{align*}
        \mathfrak{m}^\pi(\bm{x}^\pi) = \mathfrak{m}(\bm{x})
    \end{align*}
    is bijective.

steps.text:
We must show that for every $\varepsilon > 0$ and every $\bm{x}^\pi \in \mathscr{T}^\pi(X)$, there exists a $\theta > 0$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{d}_\pi(\bm{x}^\pi, \bm{y}^\pi) < \theta \implies \rho\bigl(\mathfrak{m}^\pi(\bm{x}^\pi), \mathfrak{m}^\pi(\bm{y}^\pi)\bigr) < \varepsilon.
    \end{align*}
    Fix $\varepsilon>0$ and choose $0<\theta<\min\{\varepsilon,1\}$. Suppose that $\bm{y}^\pi \in \mathscr{T}^\pi(X)$ satisfies $\mathscr{d}_\pi(\bm{x}^\pi,\bm{y}^\pi)<\theta < 1$. By Definition~\ref{defn:permuted-tuple-hausdorff-distance}, the representatives $\bm{x}$ and $\bm{y}$ must have the same length $\ell(\bm{x}) = \ell(\bm{y}) = n$. If $n = 0$, then $\bm{x}^\pi = \bm{e}^\pi = \bm{y}^\pi$, so $\rho(\mathfrak{m}^\pi(\bm{x}^\pi), \mathfrak{m}^\pi(\bm{y}^\pi)) = 0 < \varepsilon$. Assume therefore that $n > 0$. Because $\mathscr{P}_n$ is finite, the minimum in Definition~\ref{defn:permuted-tuple-hausdorff-distance} is attained by some permutation $\sigma \in \mathscr{P}_n$. Defining the permuted representative $\widetilde{\bm{y}} = \bm{y} \circ \sigma \in \bm{y}^\pi$, we have $\bar{d}(\bm{x}(i), \widetilde{\bm{y}}(i)) < \theta < 1$ for every $i \in \{1, \dots, n\}$. Since $\bar{d}$ and $d$ coincide at distances strictly below $1$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:the-standard-machinery}), it follows that
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x},\widetilde{\bm{y}})
        = \max_{i \in \{1, \dots, n\}} d\big(\bm{x}(i),\widetilde{\bm{y}}(i)\big)
        = \mathscr{d}_\pi(\bm{x}^\pi, \bm{y}^\pi) < \theta.
    \end{align*}
    By Corollary~\ref{cor:tuple-induced-measure-permutation-invariant}, we have $\mathfrak{m}^\pi(\bm{y}^\pi) = \mathfrak{m}(\bm{y}) = \mathfrak{m}(\widetilde{\bm{y}})$. Applying the proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:tuple-induced-measure-continuous} with every $\theta^\prime \in (\mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x},\widetilde{\bm{y}}), 1)$ in place of $\theta$ and taking the infimum over $\theta^\prime$ yields
    \begin{align*}
        \rho\bigl(\mathfrak{m}^\pi(\bm{x}^\pi),\mathfrak{m}^\pi(\bm{y}^\pi)\bigr)
        = \rho\bigl(\mathfrak{m}(\bm{x}),\mathfrak{m}(\widetilde{\bm{y}})\bigr)
        \leq \mathscr{d}_H(\bm{x},\widetilde{\bm{y}}) < \theta < \varepsilon,
    \end{align*}
    which proves that $\mathfrak{m}^\pi$ is continuous, as desired.

