\label{prop:weak-stationarity}
    Let $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P})$ be a probability space, $(\mathcal{H}, \langle \cdot, \cdot\rangle)$ be a real separable Hilbert space with an orthonormal basis $(e_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$, and $X = (X_t)_{t \in \mathbb{N}}$ be a sequence of $\mathcal{H}$-valued random elements with $\mathbb{E}[\lVert X_t\rVert^2] < \infty$ for all $t \in \mathbb{N}$. Then $X$ is weakly stationary if and only if the score sequence
    \begin{align*}
        (\theta_t^\infty)_{t \in \mathbb{N}} = ((\theta_{t,n})_{n\in\mathbb{N}})_{t \in \mathbb{N}} = ((\langle X_t, e_n\rangle)_{n\in\mathbb{N}})_{t \in \mathbb{N}}
    \end{align*}
    has coordinate means and cross-covariances depending only on the lag. That is, for all $n,m \in \mathbb{N}$, the score process $(\theta_{t,n})_{t \in \mathbb{N}}$ has mean $\mu_n$ independent of $t$, and
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[(\theta_{t+h,n} - \mu_n)(\theta_{t,m} - \mu_m)] = \sigma_{n,m}(h)
    \end{align*}
    depends only on $h \in \mathbb{N}_0$.

steps.text:
We must show first that $\sigma(X)=\mathcal{G}_\infty$, where
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{G}_\infty = \sigma(\theta_n, n \in \mathbb{N}),
    \end{align*}
    and second that $\mathbb{E}[Y\mid\mathcal{G}_N]\to\mathbb{E}[Y\mid\sigma(X)]$ almost surely and in $L^1$ (respectively $L^2$) as $N\to\infty$. Each score $\theta_n=\langle X,e_n\rangle$ is the composition of $X$ with a continuous linear functional. Consequently, every $\theta_n$ is $\sigma(X)$-measurable, and hence
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{G}_\infty \subseteq \sigma(X).
    \end{align*}
    To prove the reverse inclusion, fix $x\in\mathcal{H}$ and $\varepsilon>0$, and set
    \begin{align*}
        x_n = \langle x, e_n\rangle,\qquad n\in\mathbb{N}.
    \end{align*}
    Parseval's identity gives
    \begin{align*}
        \lVert X - x\rVert^2 = \sum_{n \in \mathbb{N}} (\theta_n - x_n)^2.
    \end{align*}
    For each $N\in\mathbb{N}$, the partial sum $S_N = \sum_{n = 1}^N (\theta_n - x_n)^2$ is $\mathcal{G}_N$-measurable and hence $\mathcal{G}_\infty$-measurable. Since $S_N\to\lVert X-x\rVert^2$ pointwise on $\Omega$, the squared distance $\lVert X-x\rVert^2$ is also $\mathcal{G}_\infty$-measurable. Therefore, the preimage of the open ball $B_\varepsilon(x)$ satisfies
    \begin{align*}
        X^{-1}\bigl(B_\varepsilon(x)\bigr)=\{\lVert X - x\rVert < \varepsilon\} = \{\lVert X - x\rVert^2 < \varepsilon^2\} \in \mathcal{G}_\infty.
    \end{align*}
    To pass from open balls to arbitrary open sets without invoking an uncountable union, use the separability of $\mathcal{H}$ to fix a countable dense subset $D\subseteq\mathcal{H}$. The family
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{C}=\{B_q(d)\mid d\in D,\ q\in\mathbb{Q}_{>0}\}
    \end{align*}
    is a countable basis for the norm topology of $\mathcal{H}$. By the preceding argument, $X^{-1}(C)\in\mathcal{G}_\infty$ for every $C\in\mathcal{C}$. Since every open subset of $\mathcal{H}$ is a countable union of elements of $\mathcal{C}$, its preimage under $X$ also belongs to $\mathcal{G}_\infty$. Hence $X$ is $\mathcal{G}_\infty/\mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})$-measurable, so $\sigma(X)\subseteq\mathcal{G}_\infty$. Together with the first inclusion, this yields
    \begin{align*}
        \sigma(X) = \mathcal{G}_\infty.
    \end{align*}
    Finally, since $(\mathcal{G}_N)_{N\in\mathbb{N}}$ is an increasing filtration with $\sigma\bigl(\bigcup_{N\in\mathbb{N}}\mathcal{G}_N\bigr)=\mathcal{G}_\infty$, L\'evy's upward martingale convergence theorem yields
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathcal{G}_N] \to \mathbb{E}[Y \mid \mathcal{G}_\infty] = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid \sigma(X)]
    \end{align*}
    almost surely and in $L^1$ for every $Y \in L^1(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P})$, and in $L^2$ whenever $Y\in L^2(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$, as desired.

