        =G\circ\Phi^{(\max\{n-1,0\})}\circ
        \left(\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{buy}},\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{sell}}\right)
        \restriction_{S_n}.
    \end{align*}
    Corollary~\ref{cor:iterated-single-stage-measurable} gives the measurability of $\Phi^{(\max\{n-1,0\})}$ for every $n\in\mathbb{N}_0$. Thus, $\mathsf{C}\restriction_{S_n}$ is Borel measurable for every $n\in\mathbb{N}_0$. Since the sets $(S_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}_0}$ form a countable measurable partition of $\mathscr{S}(O,\preceq)$, the measurable pasting property of the standard machinery (Theorem~\ref{thm:the-standard-machinery}) implies that $\mathsf{C}$ is Borel measurable on $\mathscr{S}(O,\preceq)$.

results.statement:
\label{defn:identifier-clearing-operator}
    Let $O$ and $\preceq$ be as in Definition~\ref{defn:price-time-priority-metric}, let $O\times\mathbb{N}$ be endowed with the product metric of Example~\ref{exmp:identifier-coordinates}, and let $h:O\to\{0,1\}\times\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}$ be the side--price--time key
    \begin{align*}
        h(((p,q,t),\mathrm{buy}))=(0,-p,t),
        \qquad h(((p,q,t),\mathrm{sell}))=(1,p,t),
    \end{align*}
    with strictly lexicographic comparison $<_{\mathrm{lex}}$, and let $\preceq_{\mathbb{N}}$ be the partial order on $O\times\mathbb{N}$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:identifier-tie-breaking} given by
    \begin{align*}
        (x, i) \preceq_{\mathbb{N}} (y, j)
        \iff (x,i)=(y,j)
        \ \lor\ h(x)<_{\mathrm{lex}}h(y)
        \ \lor\ \bigl(h(x)=h(y)\land i<j\bigr).
    \end{align*}
    This relation coincides with equality or strict lexicographic comparison of $(h(x),i)$, as in Proposition~\ref{prop:identifier-tie-breaking}; it is a partial order on the full product $O\times\mathbb{N}$ and restricts to a total order on the entries of any tuple with distinct identifiers.
    Let $\varpi:O\times\mathbb{N}\to O$, $(x,i)\mapsto x$, be the coordinate projection onto $O$, and let $\varpi_*:\mathscr{T}(O\times\mathbb{N})\to\mathscr{T}(O)$, $\varpi_*\bm{m}=\varpi\circ\bm{m}$, denote its tuple-wise extension. Identifying $O\times\mathbb{N}$ with $\bigsqcup_{\kappa\in\{\mathrm{buy},\mathrm{sell}\}} (U\times\mathbb{N})$, let $\Phi_{\mathbb{N}}$ be the single-stage clearing map on $\mathscr{S}(U\times\mathbb{N},\preceq^{\mathrm{buy}}_{\mathbb{N}})\times\mathscr{S}(U\times\mathbb{N},\preceq^{\mathrm{sell}}_{\mathbb{N}})$ defined by replacing $((p_{\bm{x}},q_{\bm{x}},t_{\bm{x}}))$ in Definition~\ref{defn:single-stage-clearing-map} with $((p_{\bm{x}},q_{\bm{x}},t_{\bm{x}}), i_{\bm{x}})$ for $\bm{x}(1) = ((p_{\bm{x}},q_{\bm{x}},t_{\bm{x}}), i_{\bm{x}}) \in U\times\mathbb{N}$. The \emph{identifier-based clearing operator}
    \begin{align*}
        \mathsf{C}_{\mathbb{N}}: \mathscr{S}(O\times\mathbb{N},\preceq_{\mathbb{N}}) \to \mathscr{S}(O\times\mathbb{N},\preceq_{\mathbb{N}})
    \end{align*}
    is defined for $\bm{m}\in\mathscr{S}(O\times\mathbb{N},\preceq_{\mathbb{N}})$ by
    \begin{align*}
        (\bm{b}^\star,\bm{s}^\star)
        &=
        \Phi_{\mathbb{N}}^{(\max\{\ell(\bm{m})-1,0\})}
        \left(\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{buy}}\bm{m},
        \bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{sell}}\bm{m}\right),\\
        \mathsf{C}_{\mathbb{N}}(\bm{m})
        &=(\bm{\iota}_{\mathrm{buy}}\bm{b}^\star)
        \oplus(\bm{\iota}_{\mathrm{sell}}\bm{s}^\star).
    \end{align*}

\label{prop:identifier-clearing-properties}
    Let $O$, $\preceq_{\mathbb{N}}$, $\varpi_*$, and $\mathsf{C}_{\mathbb{N}}$ be as in Definition~\ref{defn:identifier-clearing-operator}, and let $\mathsf{C}$ be as in Definition~\ref{defn:clearing-operator}. Then $\mathsf{C}_{\mathbb{N}}: \mathscr{S}(O\times\mathbb{N},\preceq_{\mathbb{N}}) \to \mathscr{S}(O\times\mathbb{N},\preceq_{\mathbb{N}})$ is Borel measurable, maps $\mathscr{S}(O\times\mathbb{N},\preceq_{\mathbb{N}})\cap\mathscr{T}_{\neq}(O\times\mathbb{N})$ into itself, and satisfies the commutation identity
    \begin{align}\label{eq:identifier-clearing-commutation}
        \varpi_*\mathsf{C}_{\mathbb{N}}(\bm{m})=\mathsf{C}(\varpi_*\bm{m}),\qquad \bm{m}\in\mathscr{S}(O\times\mathbb{N},\preceq_{\mathbb{N}}).
    \end{align}

\label{defn:visible-order-book}
    Let $O$ and $\preceq$ be as in Definition~\ref{defn:price-time-priority-metric}, let $\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{buy}}$ and $\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{sell}}$ be the separation operators of Definition~\ref{defn:separation-operators}, and let $\bm{\iota}_{\mathrm{buy}}$ and $\bm{\iota}_{\mathrm{sell}}$ be the retagging maps of Definition~\ref{defn:retagging}. The \emph{visible tuple space} is the subset
    \begin{align*}
        \mathscr{B} = \{\bm{m} \in \mathscr{S}(O, \preceq) \mid \max(\ell(\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{buy}} \bm{m}), \ell(\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{sell}} \bm{m})) \leq 100\},
    \end{align*}
    and the \emph{visible projection} $\Pi^{\mathscr{B}} : \mathscr{S}(O, \preceq) \to \mathscr{B}$ is defined by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:intraday-visible-projection}
        \Pi^{\mathscr{B}}(\bm{m})
        &=
        \bm{\iota}_{\mathrm{buy}}\!\left((\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{buy}} \bm{m})[\{1, \dots, 100\}]\right)
        \notag\\
        &\qquad\oplus
        \bm{\iota}_{\mathrm{sell}}\!\left((\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{sell}} \bm{m})[\{1, \dots, 100\}]\right).
    \end{align}

\label{defn:depth-restricted-step}
    Let $O$ and $\preceq$ be as in Definition~\ref{defn:price-time-priority-metric}, let $\varsigma:\mathscr{T}(O)\to\mathscr{S}(O,\preceq)$ be the minimum sorting map, let $\mathsf{C}$ be the clearing operator of Definition~\ref{defn:clearing-operator}, and let $\Pi^{\mathscr{B}}$ be the visible projection of Definition~\ref{defn:visible-order-book}. For any $\bm{m}_{t-}\in\mathscr{S}(O,\preceq)$ and any composite action $\bm{c}_t\in\mathscr{C}(O)$, define
    \begin{align*}
        \widetilde{\bm{m}}_t = \varsigma\!\left(\bm{m}_{t-} \lhd \bm{c}_t\right),
        \qquad
        \bm{m}^\prime = \mathsf{C}(\Pi^{\mathscr{B}}\widetilde{\bm{m}}_t),
    \end{align*}
    and the tail sub-tuples
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{b}^{\mathrm{latent}}_{t} &= (\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{buy}} \widetilde{\bm{m}}_t)[\operatorname{domain}(\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{buy}} \widetilde{\bm{m}}_t) \setminus \{1,\dots, 100\} ],\\
        \bm{s}^{\mathrm{latent}}_{t} &= (\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{sell}} \widetilde{\bm{m}}_t)[\operatorname{domain}(\bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{sell}} \widetilde{\bm{m}}_t) \setminus \{1,\dots, 100\} ].
    \end{align*}
    Setting
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{b}_t &= \bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{buy}} \bm{m}^\prime \oplus \bm{b}^{\mathrm{latent}}_{t},\\
        \bm{s}_t &= \bm{\Pi}_{\mathrm{sell}} \bm{m}^\prime \oplus \bm{s}^{\mathrm{latent}}_{t},
    \end{align*}
    the \emph{depth-restricted step operator} $\unlhd:\mathscr{S}(O,\preceq)\times\mathscr{C}(O)\to\mathscr{S}(O,\preceq)$ is defined by
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{m}_{t-}\unlhd\bm{c}_t = \bm{m}_t = (\bm{\iota}_{\mathrm{buy}}\bm{b}_t)
        \oplus(\bm{\iota}_{\mathrm{sell}}\bm{s}_t).
    \end{align*}

\label{defn:order-book-path-point-process}
    Let $(O,d_O)$ be a complete and separable metric space equipped with a partial order $\preceq$ and a sorted composite-action map $\unlhd:\mathscr{S}(O,\preceq)\times\mathscr{C}(O)\to\mathscr{S}(O,\preceq)$. Fix an initial state $\bm{m}_0\in\mathscr{S}(O,\preceq)$ at time $t_0=0$, and let $(\bm{c}_i)_{i=1}^n$ in $\mathscr{C}(O)$ be composite actions paired with strictly increasing times $0<t_1<\cdots< t_n\leq1$. With $\bm{m}^{(0)}=\bm{m}_0$, the \emph{iterated states} are defined recursively by
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{m}^{(i)}=\bm{m}^{(i-1)}\unlhd\bm{c}_i,
        \qquad i\in\{1,\ldots,n\},
    \end{align*}
    and the \emph{right-continuous state path} $t\mapsto\bm{m}_t$ on $[0,1]$ is defined by
    \begin{align*}
        i(t)&=\max\{i\in\{0,\ldots,n\} \mid t_i\leq t\},\\
        \bm{m}_t&=\bm{m}^{(i(t))},\qquad t\in[0,1].
    \end{align*}
    Given a probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$ and a random tuple $(\bm{t},\bm{c})=((t_1,\ldots,t_n),\allowbreak(\bm{c}_1,\ldots,\bm{c}_n))$ of strictly increasing times in $(0,1]$ and marks in $\mathscr{C}(O)$, the \emph{marked action point process} $\xi:\Omega\to\mathcal{N}([0,1]\times\mathscr{C}(O))$ is the random counting measure defined for $\omega\in\Omega$ and $E\in\mathcal{B}([0,1]\times\mathscr{C}(O))$ by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:point-process-market-flow}
        \xi(\omega, E) \overset{\mathrm{def}}{=} \xi(\omega)(E) = \sum_{i \leq n(\omega)} \delta_{(t_i(\omega), \bm{c}_i(\omega))}(E).
    \end{align}

\label{defn:block-bootstrap}
    Let $\mathscr{C}(O)$ be the composite-action space over a complete and separable metric space $(O,d_O)$, let $(\tau_j)_{j = 0}^L$ be a partition of $[0,1]$ satisfying
    \begin{align*}
        0 = \tau_0 < \tau_1 < \cdots < \tau_L = 1, 
    \end{align*}
    let $(I_{j})_{j=1}^L$ be the family of half-open intervals $I_j = (\tau_{j-1}, \tau_j]$, and set $C_j = I_j \times \mathscr{C}(O)$ for each $j\in\{1,\dots,L\}$. Given a family $(\xi_k)_{k = 1}^K$ of point processes on $[0,1]\times \mathscr{C}(O)$ formulated on a probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$, the \emph{$j$-th block-restricted point process} of $\xi_k$ is defined by
    \begin{align*}
        \xi_k^j(E) = \xi_k(E \cap C_j),\qquad E\in\mathcal{B}([0,1]\times\mathscr{C}(O)),
    \end{align*}
    so that $\xi_k^j$ retains the atoms of $\xi_k$ in $C_j$ and discards all others. For any block-index sequence $(k_j)_{j=1}^L \in \{1,\dots,K\}^L$, the \emph{block-bootstrapped point process} is defined by
    \begin{align*}
        \xi^\ast = \sum_{j = 1}^L \xi_{k_j}^j.
    \end{align*}

steps.text:
Since $\xi_1(\{0\}\times\mathscr{C}(O))=0$ almost surely and $[0,1]=\{0\}\cup\bigcup_{j=1}^L I_j$ is a disjoint union, we have $\xi_1 = \sum_{j=1}^L \xi_1^j$ almost surely. Because addition of counting measures is Borel measurable on $\mathcal{N}([0,1]\times\mathscr{C}(O))^L$, applying the summation map to both vectors yields
    \begin{align*}
        \xi^\ast = \sum_{j=1}^L \xi_{k_j}^j \stackrel{d}{=} \sum_{j=1}^L \xi_1^j = \xi_1.
    \end{align*}
    Finally, if $(k_j)_{j=1}^L$ is random and independent of $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^K$, conditioning on the realisation of $(k_j)_{j=1}^L$ and applying the deterministic result on each fibre shows that the conditional law of $\xi^\ast$ given $(k_j)_{j=1}^L$ equals the unconditional law of $\xi_1$, which completes the proof.

proof.text:
Suppose first that $(k_j)_{j=1}^L \in \{1,\dots,K\}^L$ is deterministic. Since the paths $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^K$ are mutually independent and the blocks $(\xi_k^j)_{j=1}^L$ within each path $k$ are mutually independent by assumption, the entire array of blocks $(\xi_k^j)_{1\le k\le K,\,1\le j\le L}$ is a collection of mutually independent random elements in $\mathcal{N}([0,1]\times\mathscr{C}(O))$. Because the interval index $j$ is distinct for each term in the sequence $(\xi_{k_1}^1,\dots,\xi_{k_L}^L)$, these $L$ blocks are distinct entries of the array even when path indices $k_j$ repeat, and they are therefore mutually independent. Moreover, since $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^K$ are identically distributed, $\xi_{k_j}^j$ has the same marginal distribution as $\xi_1^j$ for every $j\in\{1,\dots,L\}$. Consequently, the random vector $(\xi_{k_1}^1,\dots,\xi_{k_L}^L)$ has the same joint distribution on $\mathcal{N}([0,1]\times\mathscr{C}(O))^L$ as $(\xi_1^1,\dots,\xi_1^L)$.

    Since $\xi_1(\{0\}\times\mathscr{C}(O))=0$ almost surely and $[0,1]=\{0\}\cup\bigcup_{j=1}^L I_j$ is a disjoint union, we have $\xi_1 = \sum_{j=1}^L \xi_1^j$ almost surely. Because addition of counting measures is Borel measurable on $\mathcal{N}([0,1]\times\mathscr{C}(O))^L$, applying the summation map to both vectors yields
    \begin{align*}
        \xi^\ast = \sum_{j=1}^L \xi_{k_j}^j \stackrel{d}{=} \sum_{j=1}^L \xi_1^j = \xi_1.
    \end{align*}
    Finally, if $(k_j)_{j=1}^L$ is random and independent of $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^K$, conditioning on the realisation of $(k_j)_{j=1}^L$ and applying the deterministic result on each fibre shows that the conditional law of $\xi^\ast$ given $(k_j)_{j=1}^L$ equals the unconditional law of $\xi_1$, which completes the proof.

results.statement:
\label{prop:block-bootstrap-invariance}
    Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$ be a probability space, let $\mathscr{C}(O)$ be the composite-action space over a complete and separable metric space $(O,d_O)$, and let $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^K$ be independent and identically distributed point processes in $\mathcal{N}([0,1]\times\mathscr{C}(O))$ such that, for each $k\in\{1,\dots,K\}$, $\xi_k(\{0\}\times\mathscr{C}(O))=0$ almost surely and the block restrictions $\xi_k^1,\dots,\xi_k^L$ of Definition~\ref{defn:block-bootstrap} are mutually independent:
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{P}\left(\xi_k^1 \in A_1, \dots, \xi_k^L \in A_L\right) = \prod_{j = 1}^L \mathbb{P}\left(\xi_k^j \in A_j\right)
    \end{align*}
    for all $A_1, \dots, A_L \in \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{N}([0,1]\times \mathscr{C}(O)))$. Then, for any deterministic sequence $(k_j)_{j=1}^L\in\{1,\dots,K\}^L$, or any random sequence $(k_j)_{j=1}^L$ taking values in $\{1,\dots,K\}^L$ independently of $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^K$, the block-bootstrapped point process $\xi^\ast = \sum_{j=1}^L \xi_{k_j}^j$ satisfies $\xi^\ast \stackrel{d}{=} \xi_1$.

\label{defn:state-dependent-selection-probabilities}
    Let $\mathscr{S}$ be a state space and let $\chi:\mathscr{S}\to\mathbb{R}^d$ be a map into $d$-dimensional Euclidean space ($d\in\mathbb{N}_+$). Given a query state $\bm{m}^\ast\in\mathscr{S}$ and a collection of candidate states $(\bm{m}_k)_{k=1}^K\in\mathscr{S}^K$ ($K\in\mathbb{N}_+$), the \emph{Gaussian-kernel selection probabilities} on $\{1,\dots,K\}$ are defined by
    \begin{align*}
        \pi(k)=\frac{\exp\bigl(-\lVert\chi(\bm{m}^\ast)-\chi(\bm{m}_k)\rVert_2^2\bigr)}{\sum_{k^\prime=1}^{K}\exp\bigl(-\lVert\chi(\bm{m}^\ast)-\chi(\bm{m}_{k^\prime})\rVert_2^2\bigr)},\qquad k\in\{1,\dots,K\}.
    \end{align*}

\label{defn:ensemble-mid-rank-coverage}
    Let $\mathscr{X}$ be a set, let $f:\mathscr{X}\to\mathbb{R}$ be a real-valued functional, let $x\in\mathscr{X}$ be a reference element, and let $x^{(1)},\ldots,x^{(B)}\in\mathscr{X}$ ($B\in\mathbb{N}_+$) be an ensemble of size $B$. The \emph{ensemble mid-rank} of $x$ with respect to $f$ is defined by
    \begin{align*}
        \widehat{F}_B\left(f, x, (x^{(p)})_{p=1}^B\right) = \frac{1}{B} \sum_{p = 1}^B \left(\mathbbm{1}\left(f(x^{(p)}) < f(x)\right) + \frac{1}{2}\mathbbm{1}\left(f(x^{(p)}) = f(x)\right)\right).  
    \end{align*}
    For a nominal miscoverage level $\alpha\in(0,1)$, the \emph{discrete reference central coverage probability} is defined by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:empirical-pit-coverage-reference}
        c_B(\alpha)=\frac{\lvert\{j\in\{0,\ldots,B\} \mid \alpha/2\le j/B\le1-\alpha/2\}\rvert}{B+1}.
    \end{align}
    Given $D\in\mathbb{N}_+$ mid-ranks $u_d = \widehat{F}_B\left(f, x_d, (x^{(p)}_d)_{p=1}^B\right)$ ($d\in\{1,\ldots,D\}$), the \emph{central coverage fraction} and \emph{upper coverage fraction} are defined by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:empirical-pit-coverage}
        \mathsf{CF}(f, \alpha)&=\frac{1}{D}\sum_{d=1}^{D}
        \mathbbm{1}\left(\frac{\alpha}{2}\leq u_d\leq 1 - \frac{\alpha}{2} \right),\\
        \mathsf{UF}(f, \alpha)&=\frac{1}{D}\sum_{d=1}^{D}
        \mathbbm{1}\left(u_d\leq 1 - \alpha \right).\notag
    \end{align}

steps.text:
Taking expectations in Definition~\ref{defn:ensemble-mid-rank-coverage} by linearity of expectation gives
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[\mathsf{CF}(f,\alpha)]
        &= \frac{1}{D}\sum_{d=1}^D \mathbb{P}\!\left(\frac{\alpha}{2} \le u_d \le 1 - \frac{\alpha}{2}\right)\\
        &= \frac{\lvert\{j\in\{0,\dots,B\} \mid \alpha/2 \le j/B \le 1-\alpha/2\}\rvert}{B+1}
        = c_B(\alpha),
    \end{align*}
    and similarly
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[\mathsf{UF}(f,\alpha)]
        &= \frac{1}{D}\sum_{d=1}^D \mathbb{P}(B u_d \le B(1-\alpha))\\
        &= \frac{\lvert\{j\in\{0,\dots,B\} \mid j \le \lfloor B(1-\alpha)\rfloor\}\rvert}{B+1}
        = \frac{\lfloor B(1-\alpha)\rfloor + 1}{B+1}.
    \end{align*}
    As $B\to\infty$, the Riemann-sum counts satisfy $c_B(\alpha)\to (1-\alpha/2) - \alpha/2 = 1-\alpha$ and $(\lfloor B(1-\alpha)\rfloor + 1)/(B+1) \to 1-\alpha$, which completes the proof.

proof.text:
Fix $d\in\{1,\dots,D\}$ and $\alpha\in(0,1)$. Since $f(x_d)$ and $f(x^{(1)}_d),\dots,f(x^{(B)}_d)$ are almost surely free of ties, the indicator $\mathbbm{1}(f(x^{(p)}_d) = f(x_d))$ vanishes almost surely for every $p\in\{1,\dots,B\}$. Consequently, $B u_d = \sum_{p=1}^B \mathbbm{1}(f(x^{(p)}_d) < f(x_d))$ equals the strict rank of $f(x_d)$ among the $B$ ensemble values. By exchangeability, every strict ordering of the $B+1$ values is equally likely, so $B u_d$ is uniformly distributed on $\{0,1,\dots,B\}$, and hence $\mathbb{P}(u_d = j/B) = 1/(B+1)$ for each $j\in\{0,\dots,B\}$.

    Taking expectations in Definition~\ref{defn:ensemble-mid-rank-coverage} by linearity of expectation gives
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[\mathsf{CF}(f,\alpha)]
        &= \frac{1}{D}\sum_{d=1}^D \mathbb{P}\!\left(\frac{\alpha}{2} \le u_d \le 1 - \frac{\alpha}{2}\right)\\
        &= \frac{\lvert\{j\in\{0,\dots,B\} \mid \alpha/2 \le j/B \le 1-\alpha/2\}\rvert}{B+1}
        = c_B(\alpha),
    \end{align*}
    and similarly
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[\mathsf{UF}(f,\alpha)]
        &= \frac{1}{D}\sum_{d=1}^D \mathbb{P}(B u_d \le B(1-\alpha))\\
        &= \frac{\lvert\{j\in\{0,\dots,B\} \mid j \le \lfloor B(1-\alpha)\rfloor\}\rvert}{B+1}
        = \frac{\lfloor B(1-\alpha)\rfloor + 1}{B+1}.
    \end{align*}
    As $B\to\infty$, the Riemann-sum counts satisfy $c_B(\alpha)\to (1-\alpha/2) - \alpha/2 = 1-\alpha$ and $(\lfloor B(1-\alpha)\rfloor + 1)/(B+1) \to 1-\alpha$, which completes the proof.

results.statement:
\label{prop:midrank-coverage-expectations}
    Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$ be a probability space, let $\mathscr{X}$ be a measurable space, let $f:\mathscr{X}\to\mathbb{R}$ be a measurable functional, and let $(x_d,x_d^{(1)},\allowbreak\dots,\allowbreak x_d^{(B)})$ ($d\in\{1,\dots,D\}$, $D,B\in\mathbb{N}_+$) be $\mathscr{X}^{B+1}$-valued random elements. Suppose that for each $d\in\{1,\dots,D\}$, the real-valued random variables $\bigl(f(x_d), f(x^{(1)}_d),\allowbreak \dots,\allowbreak f(x^{(B)}_d)\bigr)$ are exchangeable and almost surely free of ties. Then, for every $\alpha\in(0,1)$, the coverage fractions of Definition~\ref{defn:ensemble-mid-rank-coverage} satisfy
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[\mathsf{CF}(f,\alpha)] = c_B(\alpha)
        \qquad\text{and}\qquad
        \mathbb{E}[\mathsf{UF}(f,\alpha)] = \frac{\lfloor B(1-\alpha)\rfloor + 1}{B+1},
    \end{align*}
    and both expectations converge to $1-\alpha$ as $B\to\infty$.

\label{defn:parser-inner-outer-programs}
    Let $n,m,N,M\in\mathbb{N}_+$, let $\mathcal{I}=(I_k)_{k=1}^N$ be a partition of $\{1,\dots,n\}$ associated with strictly increasing scalars $\sigma_1<\cdots<\sigma_N$ in $\mathbb{R}$, and let $\mathcal{J}=(J_l)_{l=1}^M$ be a partition of $\{1,\dots,m\}$ associated with strictly increasing scalars $\tilde{\sigma}_1<\cdots<\tilde{\sigma}_M$ in $\mathbb{R}$. Let $\mathcal{A},\mathcal{R}\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}$ be disjoint subsets, let $(p_i)_{i=1}^n\in\mathbb{R}^n$, $(\Delta q_i)_{i\in\mathcal{R}}\in(0,\infty)^{\mathcal{R}}$, and $(\kappa_i)_{i=1}^n\in\{+1,-1\}^n$, let $(\tilde{p}_j)_{j=1}^m\in\mathbb{R}^m$ and $(\tilde{q}_j)_{j=1}^m\in(0,\infty)^m$, and let $\varepsilon_p\ge 0$ and $\delta_t>0$. For any pair $(I_k,J_l)\in\mathcal{I}\times\mathcal{J}$ with $R=I_k\cap\mathcal{R}$, $A=I_k\cap\mathcal{A}$, and $\lvert\sigma_k-\tilde{\sigma}_l\rvert\le\delta_t$, the \emph{block-pair subproblem score} $\mathsf{Match}_{I_k,J_l}$ is the optimal value of the mixed-integer linear program
    \begin{align}\label{eq:intraday-inner-match-main}
        \mathsf{Match}_{I_k,J_l} &
        =\max_{x,y,\beta}
          \left\{\sum_{\substack{i\in R\\j\in J_l}}x_{i,j}\tilde{q}_j
          +\sum_{\substack{u\in A\\j\in J_l}}y_{u,j}\tilde{q}_j\right\}\\
        \text{s.t.}\quad
        & \sum_{i\in R}x_{i,j}\leq\beta_j
          \tag*{$\forall j\in J_l,$}\\
        & \sum_{u\in A}y_{u,j}\leq1-\beta_j
          \tag*{$\forall j\in J_l,$}\\
        & \sum_{j\in J_l}x_{i,j}\tilde{q}_j\leq\Delta q_i
          \tag*{$\forall i\in R,$}\\
        & x_{i,j}=0
          \tag*{$\forall i\in R,\ j\in J_l:\ \kappa_i=+1\land \tilde{p}_j>p_i+\varepsilon_p,$}\\
        & x_{i,j}=0
          \tag*{$\forall i\in R,\ j\in J_l:\ \kappa_i=-1\land \tilde{p}_j<p_i-\varepsilon_p,$}\\
        & y_{u,j}=0
          \tag*{$\forall u\in A,\ j\in J_l:\ \lvert\tilde{p}_j-p_u\rvert>\varepsilon_p,$}\\
        & x_{i,j}\in\{0,1\}
          \tag*{$\forall i\in R,\ j\in J_l,$}\\
        & y_{u,j}\in\{0,1\}
          \tag*{$\forall u\in A,\ j\in J_l,$}\\
        & \beta_j\in\{0,1\}
          \tag*{$\forall j\in J_l,$}
    \end{align}
    and $\mathsf{Match}_{I_k,J_l}=-\infty$ whenever $\lvert\sigma_k-\tilde{\sigma}_l\rvert>\delta_t$. The \emph{dynamic-programming value array} $(V_{k,l})_{0\le k\le N,\,0\le l\le M}$ is defined recursively by the boundary conditions $V_{0,0}=0$, $V_{0,l}=0$ for all $l\in\{1,\dots,M\}$, and $V_{k,0}=0$ for all $k\in\{1,\dots,N\}$, together with the Bellman recursion, for $k\in\{1,\dots,N\}$ and $l\in\{1,\dots,M\}$,
    \begin{align}\label{eq:intraday-outer-dp-main}
        V_{k,l}
        =\max\bigl\{V_{k-1,l},\;V_{k,l-1},\;V_{k-1,l-1}+\mathsf{Match}_{I_k,J_l}\bigr\}.
    \end{align}

\label{defn:aa-encoder}
    Let $M=41$ be the sequence length, let $h=64$ be the embedding dimension, let $W_{\mathrm{e}}\in\mathbb{R}^{64\times4}$, $W_{\mathrm{K}},W_{\mathrm{Q}},W_{\mathrm{V}}\in\mathbb{R}^{64\times64}$, $\bm{\pi}_1,\dots,\bm{\pi}_{41}\in\mathbb{R}^{64}$, $\bm{\omega}\in\mathbb{R}^{64}$, and $W_{\mathrm{o}}\in\mathbb{R}^{32\times 68}$, and let $F:\mathbb{R}^{64}\to\mathbb{R}^{128}\to\mathbb{R}^{64}$ be a two-layer position-wise network with a Gaussian error linear unit (GELU) activation. For an input tuple of vectors $(\varphi_1,\dots,\varphi_{41})\in(\mathbb{R}^4)^{41}$ and a scalar $m_w\in\mathbb{R}$, define the embedded vectors
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{z}_i=W_{\mathrm{e}}\varphi_i+\bm{\pi}_i,\qquad i=1,\dots,41,
    \end{align*}
    the self-attention updates
    \begin{align*}
        a_{ij}=\frac{\exp\bigl(\langle W_{\mathrm{K}}\bm{z}_j,W_{\mathrm{Q}}\bm{z}_i\rangle/\sqrt{h}\bigr)}{\sum_{l=1}^{41}\exp\bigl(\langle W_{\mathrm{K}}\bm{z}_l,W_{\mathrm{Q}}\bm{z}_i\rangle/\sqrt{h}\bigr)},\qquad
        \bm{z}_i'=\mathrm{LN}\Bigl(\bm{z}_i+\sum_{j=1}^{41}a_{ij}W_{\mathrm{V}}\bm{z}_j\Bigr),
    \end{align*}
    where $\mathrm{LN}:\mathbb{R}^{64}\to\mathbb{R}^{64}$ denotes coordinatewise layer normalisation, and the post-feedforward vectors $\bm{y}_i=\mathrm{LN}(\bm{z}_i'+F(\bm{z}_i'))$. With pooling weights $\alpha_i=\exp(\bm{\omega}^{\top}\bm{y}_i)/\allowbreak\sum_{l=1}^{41}\exp(\bm{\omega}^{\top}\bm{y}_l)$ and harmonic features
    \begin{align*}
        \bm{c}(m_w)=\bigl(\sin\tfrac{2\pi m_w}{1440},\cos\tfrac{2\pi m_w}{1440},\sin\tfrac{4\pi m_w}{1440},\cos\tfrac{4\pi m_w}{1440}\bigr)\in\mathbb{R}^4,
    \end{align*}
    the \emph{single-layer self-attention encoder} output is defined by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:aa-encoder}
        E_\theta\bigl((\varphi_i)_{i=1}^{41},m_w\bigr)=W_{\mathrm{o}}\Bigl[\textstyle\sum_{i=1}^{41}\alpha_i\bm{y}_i\,;\,\bm{c}(m_w)\Bigr]\in\mathbb{R}^{32}.
    \end{align}

\label{defn:aa-retrieval-policy}
    Let $\mathcal{C}_{d,w}$ be a non-empty finite index set of pairs $e=(d_e,w_e)$, let $\bm{e}_w\in\mathbb{R}^{32}$ be a query vector, let $(\bm{k}_e)_{e\in\mathcal{C}_{d,w}}$ in $\mathbb{R}^{32}$ be a family of key vectors, let $(b^{\mathrm{win}}_\delta)_{\delta\in\{-1,0,1\}}\in\mathbb{R}^3$ and $(b^{\mathrm{age}}_r)_{r\in\{1,2,3,4\}}\in\mathbb{R}^4$ be bias vectors, and let $a:\mathbb{Z}\to\{1,2,3,4\}$ be an index map. For each $e=(d_e,w_e)\in\mathcal{C}_{d,w}$ with $w_e-w\in\{-1,0,1\}$, the \emph{retrieval score} is defined by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:aa-score}
        s_w(e)=-\lVert\bm{e}_w-\bm{k}_e\rVert_2^2+b^{\mathrm{win}}_{w_e-w}+b^{\mathrm{age}}_{a(d-d_e)},
    \end{align}
    and the \emph{retrieval policy} is the probability distribution on $\mathcal{C}_{d,w}$ defined by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:aa-policy}
        \pi_w(e)=\frac{\exp s_w(e)}{\sum_{e'\in\mathcal{C}_{d,w}}\exp s_w(e')},\qquad e\in\mathcal{C}_{d,w}.
    \end{align}

steps.text:
Expanding the squared Euclidean norm in the score $s_w(e)$ of~\eqref{eq:aa-score} gives
    \begin{align*}
        -\lVert\bm{e}_w-\bm{k}_e\rVert_2^2 = 2\langle\bm{e}_w,\bm{k}_e\rangle - \lVert\bm{e}_w\rVert_2^2 - \lVert\bm{k}_e\rVert_2^2.
    \end{align*}
    Substituting this identity into~\eqref{eq:aa-policy} and factoring $\exp(-\lVert\bm{e}_w\rVert_2^2)$ out of both the numerator and the denominator, since $\lVert\bm{e}_w\rVert_2^2$ is independent of the index $e\in\mathcal{C}_{d,w}$, yields the stated attention expression.

proof.text:
Expanding the squared Euclidean norm in the score $s_w(e)$ of~\eqref{eq:aa-score} gives
    \begin{align*}
        -\lVert\bm{e}_w-\bm{k}_e\rVert_2^2 = 2\langle\bm{e}_w,\bm{k}_e\rangle - \lVert\bm{e}_w\rVert_2^2 - \lVert\bm{k}_e\rVert_2^2.
    \end{align*}
    Substituting this identity into~\eqref{eq:aa-policy} and factoring $\exp(-\lVert\bm{e}_w\rVert_2^2)$ out of both the numerator and the denominator, since $\lVert\bm{e}_w\rVert_2^2$ is independent of the index $e\in\mathcal{C}_{d,w}$, yields the stated attention expression.

results.statement:
\label{prop:aa-attention-equivalence}
    Under the assumptions and notation of Definition~\ref{defn:aa-retrieval-policy}, for any query vector $\bm{e}_w\in\mathbb{R}^{32}$ and candidate keys $(\bm{k}_e)_{e\in\mathcal{C}_{d,w}}$ in $\mathbb{R}^{32}$, the probability distribution $\pi_w$ of~\eqref{eq:aa-policy} coincides with the softmax weights of single-head dot-product attention with query $\bm{e}_w$, keys $\bm{k}_e$, temperature $\tau = 1/2$, and additive key bias $-\lVert\bm{k}_e\rVert_2^2 + b^{\mathrm{win}}_{w_e-w} + b^{\mathrm{age}}_{a(d-d_e)}$:
    \begin{align*}
        \pi_w(e) = \frac{\exp\bigl(\langle\bm{e}_w,\bm{k}_e\rangle / (1/2) - \lVert\bm{k}_e\rVert_2^2 + b^{\mathrm{win}}_{w_e-w} + b^{\mathrm{age}}_{a(d-d_e)}\bigr)}{\sum_{e'\in\mathcal{C}_{d,w}}\exp\bigl(\langle\bm{e}_w,\bm{k}_{e'}\rangle / (1/2) - \lVert\bm{k}_{e'}\rVert_2^2 + b^{\mathrm{win}}_{w_{e'}-w} + b^{\mathrm{age}}_{a(d-d_{e'})}\bigr)}.
    \end{align*}

\label{defn:aa-training-energy-score}
    Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a non-empty finite index set, let $(\bm{y}_e)_{e\in\mathcal{C}}$ in $\mathbb{R}^{33}$ be a family of outcome vectors, let $\bm{y}_q\in\mathbb{R}^{33}$ be a target outcome vector, and let $\pi\in\Delta(\mathcal{C})=\{\pi'\in[0,1]^{\mathcal{C}} \mid \sum_{e\in\mathcal{C}}\pi'(e)=1\}$ be a discrete probability distribution on $\mathcal{C}$. The \emph{discrete mixture energy score} of $\pi$ at $\bm{y}_q$ is defined by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:aa-es-mixture}
        \mathsf{ES}(\pi;\bm{y}_q)=\sum_{e\in\mathcal{C}}\pi(e)\,\lVert\bm{y}_e-\bm{y}_q\rVert_2-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{e,e'\in\mathcal{C}}\pi(e)\pi(e')\,\lVert\bm{y}_e-\bm{y}_{e'}\rVert_2.
    \end{align}

\label{defn:intraday-ensemble-energy-score}
    Let $d,B\in\mathbb{N}_+$, let $\bm{y}_t\in\mathbb{R}^d$ be an observation vector, and let $\bm{y}_t^{(1)},\dots,\bm{y}_t^{(B)}\in\mathbb{R}^d$ be an ensemble of size $B$. For $B\ge 2$, the \emph{finite-ensemble energy score estimator} is defined by
    \begin{align}\label{eq:intraday-energy-score}
        \mathsf{ES}_t
        =\frac{1}{B}\sum_{p=1}^{B}\lVert\bm{y}_t^{(p)}-\bm{y}_t\rVert_2
        -\frac{1}{2B(B-1)}\sum_{\substack{p,p'=1\\p\ne p'}}^{B}
            \lVert\bm{y}_t^{(p)}-\bm{y}_t^{(p')}\rVert_2,
    \end{align}
    and for $B=1$, the second term is set to zero, so that $\mathsf{ES}_t=\lVert\bm{y}_t^{(1)}-\bm{y}_t\rVert_2$. In the scalar case $d=1$, for $m_t\in\mathbb{R}$ and $m_t^{(1)},\dots,m_t^{(B)}\in\mathbb{R}$, the \emph{finite-ensemble continuous ranked probability score (CRPS) estimator} is defined by~\eqref{eq:intraday-energy-score} with $\lvert m_t^{(p)}-m_t\rvert$ and $\lvert m_t^{(p)}-m_t^{(p')}\rvert$ in place of the Euclidean norms.

\label{defn:se-mandate}
Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$ be a probability space, let $K \in \mathbb{N}_+$, let $\mathsf{TV} > 0$, and let $\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_K$ be independent and identically distributed standard normal random variables on $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P})$. The \emph{pathwise total-variation-normalised Gaussian walk} $(w_k)_{k=0}^K$ is the stochastic process defined almost surely by
\begin{align*}
    w_0=0,\qquad
    w_k=\mathsf{TV}\,\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{k}\xi_j}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\lvert\xi_j\rvert}\quad (k=1,\ldots,K),
\end{align*}
equipped with its natural filtration $\mathcal{G}_k=\sigma(w_0,\ldots,w_k)$ ($k=0,\ldots,K$).

steps.text:
We must show that $\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lvert w_k-w_{k-1}\rvert=\mathsf{TV}$ almost surely and that for every $k\in\{0,\ldots,K-1\}$, the random variable $w_k$ is $\mathcal{G}_k$-measurable and integrable, and that
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}[w_{k+1}-w_k\mid\mathcal{G}_k]=0\qquad\text{almost surely.}
\end{align*}
First, note that $S(\bm{\xi})=\sum_{j=1}^{K}\lvert\xi_j\rvert>0$ almost surely because the $\xi_j$ are continuous random variables. Summing the absolute increments gives $\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lvert w_k-w_{k-1}\rvert=\mathsf{TV}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lvert\xi_k\rvert/S(\bm{\xi})=\mathsf{TV}$ almost surely. Moreover, for every $k\in\{0,\ldots,K\}$, the triangle inequality gives
\begin{align*}
    \lvert w_k\rvert\le\mathsf{TV}\,\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{k}\lvert\xi_j\rvert}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\lvert\xi_j\rvert}\le\mathsf{TV}\qquad\text{almost surely,}
\end{align*}
so $w_k$ is bounded and hence integrable, and $\lvert w_{k+1}-w_k\rvert\le\mathsf{TV}$. By definition of $\mathcal{G}_k$, each $w_k$ is $\mathcal{G}_k$-measurable.

To establish the martingale identity, it suffices by definition of conditional expectation to show that for every bounded Borel-measurable function $h\colon\mathbb{R}^{k+1}\to\mathbb{R}$,
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}\bigl[(w_{k+1}-w_k)\,h(w_0,\ldots,w_k)\bigr]=0.
\end{align*}
To this end, fix $k\in\{0,\ldots,K-1\}$ and define the sign-flipping map $T_{k+1}\colon\mathbb{R}^K\to\mathbb{R}^K$ on the underlying Gaussian vector $\bm{\xi}=(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_K)$ by
\begin{align*}
    T_{k+1}(\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_k,\xi_{k+1},\xi_{k+2},\ldots,\xi_K)
    = (\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_k,-\xi_{k+1},\xi_{k+2},\ldots,\xi_K).
\end{align*}
Because the components of $\bm{\xi}$ are independent and $\xi_{k+1}\stackrel{d}{=}-\xi_{k+1}$, the transformed vector $\tilde{\bm{\xi}}=T_{k+1}(\bm{\xi})$ has the same joint distribution as $\bm{\xi}$. Moreover, since $\lvert-\xi_{k+1}\rvert=\lvert\xi_{k+1}\rvert$, the denominator $S(\bm{\xi})=\sum_{j=1}^{K}\lvert\xi_j\rvert$ satisfies $S(\tilde{\bm{\xi}})=S(\bm{\xi})$.

For every $r\in\{0,\ldots,k\}$, the numerator $\sum_{j=1}^{r}\xi_j$ does not involve $\xi_{k+1}$, so $w_r(\tilde{\bm{\xi}})=w_r(\bm{\xi})$. By contrast, the $(k+1)$-th increment satisfies
\begin{align*}
    (w_{k+1}-w_k)(\tilde{\bm{\xi}})=\mathsf{TV}\,\frac{-\xi_{k+1}}{S(\bm{\xi})}=-(w_{k+1}-w_k)(\bm{\xi}).
\end{align*}
Consequently, equality in law between $\bm{\xi}$ and $\tilde{\bm{\xi}}$ yields
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}\bigl[(w_{k+1}-w_k)\,h(w_0,\ldots,w_k)\bigr]
    = \mathbb{E}\bigl[-(w_{k+1}-w_k)\,h(w_0,\ldots,w_k)\bigr],
\end{align*}
which implies $\mathbb{E}\bigl[(w_{k+1}-w_k)\,h(w_0,\ldots,w_k)\bigr]=0$, as desired.

proof.text:
We must show that $\sum_{k=1}^{K}\lvert w_k-w_{k-1}\rvert=\mathsf{TV}$ almost surely and that for every $k\in\{0,\ldots,K-1\}$, the random variable $w_k$ is $\mathcal{G}_k$-measurable and integrable, and that
\begin{align*}
