results.statement:
\label{defn:fixed-grid-representative}
Fix $m=500$, set $h=1/m$, and define the grid nodes $u_j=j/m$ for $j\in\{-m,\ldots,m\}$ and the grid cells $K_j=[u_{j-1},u_j]$ for $j\in\{-m+1,\ldots,m\}$, yielding $1{,}001$ nodes ($500$ on each side of $u_0=0$) and $1{,}000$ cells of width $h$. For any selected square-integrable function pair $F=(\psi,\phi)\in\mathcal{L}^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$, with the pointwise values of $\psi$ at the nodes $(u_j)_{j=-m}^{m}$ included as part of the input, the \emph{numerical representative} of $F$ is the pair of vectors
\begin{align}\label{eq:501-node-representative}
\begin{gathered}
\mathsf{A}(F)=\bigl((a_j)_{j=-m}^{m},(c_j)_{j=-m+1}^{m}\bigr)\in\mathbb{R}^{1001}\times\mathbb{R}^{1000},\\
a_j=\psi(u_j),\qquad c_j=\frac{1}{h}\int_{K_j}\phi(u)\,\mathrm{d}u.
\end{gathered}
\end{align}
Because $a_j=\psi(u_j)$ evaluates $\psi$ on the finite node set, the quantity samples depend on the selected representative $\psi$, whereas the price cell averages $c_j$ are invariant under almost-everywhere modifications of $\phi$. In our curve application, $\psi=\psi_f$ is the continuous quantity coordinate of Definition~\ref{defn:parametrisation-operator} and Section~\ref{sec:piecewise-linear-curves}.

\label{defn:isotonic-decoding-grid}
Fix $m=500$, set $h=1/m$, and let $\underline{p}<\overline{p}$ be real numbers. With weights $w_j=h$ for $\lvert j\rvert<m$ and $w_{\pm m}=h/2$, the \emph{isotonic coordinate fits} of $\widetilde{z}=(\widetilde{a},\widetilde{c})\in\mathbb{R}^{1001}\times\mathbb{R}^{1000}$, where $\widetilde{a}=(\widetilde{a}_j)_{j=-m}^{m}$ and $\widetilde{c}=(\widetilde{c}_j)_{j=-m+1}^{m}$, are defined by
\begin{align}
\bm{q}(\widetilde{z})
&=\underset{\substack{x\in\mathbb{R}^{1001}\\x_{-m}\leq\cdots\leq x_m}}{\arg\min}
\sum_{j=-m}^{m}w_j\bigl(x_j-\widetilde{a}_j\bigr)^2,
\label{eq:quantity-pava}\\
\bm{p}(\widetilde{z})
&=\underset{\substack{y\in\mathbb{R}^{1000}\\\underline{p}\leq y_{-m+1}\leq\cdots\leq y_m\leq\overline{p}}}{\arg\min}
\sum_{j=-m+1}^{m}\bigl(y_j-\widetilde{c}_j\bigr)^2,
\label{eq:price-pava}
\end{align}
and the \emph{decoded representative} is $\bigl(\bm{q}(\widetilde{z}),\bm{p}(\widetilde{z})\bigr)$. Writing $q_j(z)=(\bm{q}(z))_j$, the \emph{grid admissibility domain} is
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{grid}}=\bigl\{z\in\mathbb{R}^{1001}\times\mathbb{R}^{1000}\;\big|\;q_{-m}(z)\leq0\leq q_m(z)\bigr\}.
\end{align*}

\label{prop:app-isotonic-properties}
Let $m=500$, $h=1/m$, and $\underline{p}<\overline{p}$ be as in Definition~\ref{defn:isotonic-decoding-grid}. For every $\widetilde{z}=(\widetilde{a},\widetilde{c})\in\mathbb{R}^{1001}\times\mathbb{R}^{1000}$, the optimisation problems~\eqref{eq:quantity-pava} and~\eqref{eq:price-pava} admit unique solutions $\bm{q}(\widetilde{z})$ and $\bm{p}(\widetilde{z})$. If $z=(a,c)=\mathsf{A}(F)$ is the numerical representative of a selected function pair $F=(\psi,\phi)\in\mathcal{L}^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$ as in Definition~\ref{defn:fixed-grid-representative} such that $\psi$ and $\phi$ are non-decreasing and $\phi([-1,1])\subseteq[\underline{p},\overline{p}]$, then $\bigl(\bm{q}(z),\bm{p}(z)\bigr)=z$, and the second coordinate satisfies
\begin{align*}
\sum_{j=-m+1}^{m}h\,\bigl((\bm{p}(\widetilde{z}))_j-c_j\bigr)^2
\leq
\sum_{j=-m+1}^{m}h\,\bigl(\widetilde{c}_j-c_j\bigr)^2.
\end{align*}

\label{defn:numerical-inner-product}
Fix $m=500$ and set $h=1/m$. For vectors $z=(a,c)$ and $z'=(a',c')$ in $\mathbb{R}^{1001}\times\mathbb{R}^{1000}$, where $a=(a_j)_{j=-m}^{m}$ and $c=(c_j)_{j=-m+1}^{m}$, the \emph{numerical inner product} is defined by
\begin{align}\label{eq:numerical-inner-product}
\begin{aligned}
\langle z,z'\rangle_{\mathrm{num}}&{}=\sum_{j=-m+1}^{m}\frac{h}{6}\bigl(2a_{j-1}a'_{j-1}+a_{j-1}a'_j+a_ja'_{j-1}+2a_ja'_j\bigr)\\
&\quad{}+\sum_{j=-m+1}^{m}h\,c_jc'_j,
\end{aligned}
\end{align}
and the induced \emph{numerical norm} is $\lVert z\rVert_{\mathrm{num}}=\langle z,z\rangle_{\mathrm{num}}^{1/2}$.

\label{prop:app-numerical-inner-product-exact}
Fix $m=500$, set $h=1/m$, and let $u_j=j/m$ for $j\in\{-m,\ldots,m\}$. For each $z=(a,c)\in\mathbb{R}^{1001}\times\mathbb{R}^{1000}$, let $F_z=(\phi_a,\pi_c)\in L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)$ be the function pair whose first coordinate $\phi_a$ interpolates linearly between $(u_{j-1},a_{j-1})$ and $(u_j,a_j)$ on each cell $K_j=[u_{j-1},u_j]$ and whose second coordinate $\pi_c$ equals $c_j$ on $K_j$. Then, for all $z,z'\in\mathbb{R}^{1001}\times\mathbb{R}^{1000}$,
\begin{align*}
\langle z,z'\rangle_{\mathrm{num}}=\langle F_z,F_{z'}\rangle_{L^2([-1,1];\mathbb{R}^2)}.
\end{align*}

{kunen2014set}}]\label{axiom:existence}\index{axioms!existence}
    There is a set. That is,
    \begin{align*}
        \exists x(x = x).
    \end{align*}

{kunen2014set}}]\label{axiom:extensionality}\index{axioms!extensionality}
    Two sets are equal if they have the same elements. That is,
    \begin{align*}
        \forall x \forall y( \forall z(z \in x \iff z \in y) \implies x = y).
    \end{align*}

{kunen2014set}}]\label{axiom:foundation}\index{axioms!foundation}
    Every non-empty set has an element from which it is disjoint. That is,
    \begin{align*}
        \forall x\left(\exists y(y \in x) \implies \exists y\left(y \in x \land \neg\exists z(z \in x \land z \in y)\right)\right).
    \end{align*}

{kunen2014set}}]\label{defn:subset}
    Let $X$ and $Y$ be sets. If
    \begin{align*}
        \forall x( x\in Y \implies x \in X),
    \end{align*}
    we write $Y \subseteq X$ and we say $Y$ is a subset of $X$. Moreover, if
    \begin{align*}
        \forall x( x\in Y \implies x\in X) \land (\exists x \in X:x\notin Y),
    \end{align*}
    we write $Y \subsetneq X$ and say that $Y$ is a strict subset of $X$.

{kunen2014set}}]\label{axiom:scheme-of-comprehension}\index{axioms!separation}
    For any formula whose free variables are among $y, z, w_1,\dots, w_n$, say, $\phi \equiv \phi(y, z, w_1, \dots, w_n)$, the corresponding axiom is
    \begin{align*}
        \forall z \forall w_1, \dots, w_n \exists x \forall y (y \in x \iff (y \in z \land \phi)).
    \end{align*}

steps.text:
We must show that there exists a set containing no elements and that any two such sets are equal. By the \hyperref[axiom:existence]{Axiom of Existence}, there exists a set $x$. By the \hyperref[axiom:scheme-of-comprehension]{Axiom Scheme of Separation}, the collection
    \begin{align*}
        \{z \in x \mid z \neq z\}
    \end{align*}
    is a set with no elements. Finally, by the \hyperref[axiom:extensionality]{Axiom of Extensionality}, all sets that have the same elements are equal. Because any two sets with no elements vacuously have the same elements, such a set is unique, as desired.

proof.text:
We must show that there exists a set containing no elements and that any two such sets are equal. By the \hyperref[axiom:existence]{Axiom of Existence}, there exists a set $x$. By the \hyperref[axiom:scheme-of-comprehension]{Axiom Scheme of Separation}, the collection
    \begin{align*}
        \{z \in x \mid z \neq z\}
    \end{align*}
    is a set with no elements. Finally, by the \hyperref[axiom:extensionality]{Axiom of Extensionality}, all sets that have the same elements are equal. Because any two sets with no elements vacuously have the same elements, such a set is unique, as desired.

steps.text:
We argue by contradiction. Suppose that $\exists y \forall x( x \in y)$. By the \hyperref[axiom:scheme-of-comprehension]{Axiom Scheme of Separation}, we may define the subset
    \begin{align*}
        r = \{x \in y \mid x \not \in x\}.
    \end{align*}
    Since $r \in y$ by the universal-set hypothesis, we may test whether $r \in r$. By definition of $r$, this gives
    \begin{align*}
        r \in r \iff r \notin r,
    \end{align*}
    which is impossible. Hence, no universal set exists.

proof.text:
We argue by contradiction. Suppose that $\exists y \forall x( x \in y)$. By the \hyperref[axiom:scheme-of-comprehension]{Axiom Scheme of Separation}, we may define the subset
    \begin{align*}
        r = \{x \in y \mid x \not \in x\}.
    \end{align*}
    Since $r \in y$ by the universal-set hypothesis, we may test whether $r \in r$. By definition of $r$, this gives
    \begin{align*}
        r \in r \iff r \notin r,
    \end{align*}
    which is impossible. Hence, no universal set exists.

